WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:04.087
[crickets chirp]

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.476
[gentle instrumental music]

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:46.880
[horns honk]
[traffic hums]

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.769
[Jeff Bridges] This Earth
was here before us,

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.104
and it will be here
long after we're gone.

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:14.574
Every living thing on
it has evolved together,

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:16.076
over eons of time.

00:01:21.623 --> 00:01:24.584
[whale sings]

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.961
And although we are
part of the web of life,

00:01:29.964 --> 00:01:34.844
because we see it, we
think we stand above it.

00:01:41.976 --> 00:01:43.311
From all that nature gave us,

00:01:43.353 --> 00:01:46.022
we have made a new
world out of wilderness,

00:01:48.191 --> 00:01:50.026
and built great civilizations.

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:54.948
It seemed there was
nothing we could not do.

00:01:56.324 --> 00:01:59.452
Even the sky itself
was not the limit.

00:01:59.494 --> 00:02:01.037
[Man] It's great to
be back in space.

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:03.998
[Jeff] We see the symptoms of
a reality we didn't expect.

00:02:07.043 --> 00:02:09.963
Have we reached the limitations
of our human nature?

00:02:11.756 --> 00:02:14.217
Is this the end of
the line for us?

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:19.472
It's hard to tell from
our current point of view,

00:02:19.514 --> 00:02:22.350
living here in
the future's past.

00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:23.685
[explosions]

00:02:38.408 --> 00:02:39.868
The world that we live in

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:43.454
is just this
shockingly blue sphere.

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:44.831
When you see it in space,

00:02:44.873 --> 00:02:46.374
you understand there's a planet

00:02:46.416 --> 00:02:49.836
that's bowling around the
sun, turning on its axis.

00:02:49.878 --> 00:02:52.964
The oceans, clouds,
mountains, forests.

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:54.507
You can see it
all from up there.

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:57.802
You can see over a thousand
miles in any direction,

00:02:57.844 --> 00:03:01.848
and it's all moving underneath
you at five miles per second.

00:03:03.391 --> 00:03:05.059
When you look at the horizon,

00:03:05.101 --> 00:03:10.106
you see a very, very thin little
blue ribbon of atmosphere,

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:12.400
and it really
brings home to you,

00:03:12.442 --> 00:03:14.694
it was a shock to me and
I'm a scientist, okay,

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:17.822
I thought I had all the scales
worked out, intellectually,

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:21.034
but it was a shock to me to
see how thin the atmosphere is

00:03:21.075 --> 00:03:25.038
and how obviously it's easily
affected by what we do.

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:30.210
This is the great
challenge of our time

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:35.131
dealing with the human
impact on the environment

00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:36.799
and what it means in terms

00:03:36.841 --> 00:03:38.635
of our civilization
going forward.

00:03:40.428 --> 00:03:43.723
We cut all the trees,
we kill all the fish.

00:03:44.641 --> 00:03:47.602
We consume and consume.

00:03:47.769 --> 00:03:50.939
So much ecological writing,
you know, just open the paper,

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.565
is about the end of the world.

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:54.859
As in, it's starting, like,
when's it going to start?

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:59.864
I wonder, three, two,
one, and the concept "world"

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:02.408
as a sort of way
of distinguishing

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:06.162
between human beings and
everything else, has evaporated.

00:04:09.332 --> 00:04:11.918
This is the only
place that we know,

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:15.421
out of this whole
universe, that we can live.

00:04:17.298 --> 00:04:19.634
Life is messy.

00:04:19.676 --> 00:04:22.762
We are changing multiple aspects
of the Earth environment.

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:26.140
Predicting all the effects of
it is incredibly complicated.

00:04:26.182 --> 00:04:27.684
What do we do?

00:04:27.725 --> 00:04:32.313
We've been standing back
and watching nature decline

00:04:32.355 --> 00:04:33.690
before our very eyes.

00:04:33.731 --> 00:04:35.733
It's on everyone's mind

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:38.778
is why are why not
doing more in response?

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.780
We've used our abilities

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.616
to build a huge technological
society that's very advanced

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:46.202
but in our essence,
we're still animals,

00:04:46.244 --> 00:04:49.539
and one of the problems
is that, as animals,

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:52.458
we have certain emotions
and certain tendencies

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:56.796
that are counterproductive
in our great, complex society.

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:01.301
Spinoza, the
philosopher, has said,

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:03.761
"Man doesn't really understand
all the unconscious processes

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:05.388
"which actually really control

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.473
"their conscious
behaviors and thoughts."

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.146
[Jeff] Perhaps the
solutions we're looking for

00:05:13.187 --> 00:05:18.526
start in us, in our genes,
our subconscious motivations,

00:05:20.028 --> 00:05:21.904
our most primal instincts.

00:05:24.449 --> 00:05:28.453
We are the most flexible
species ever evolved,

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:29.329
I would think.

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:33.374
And when you see people
thriving in the Arctic,

00:05:33.416 --> 00:05:36.794
and you see people thriving
in the Kalahari Desert,

00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:39.839
and you see people thriving
in the Amazon Rainforest,

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:41.758
these are titanic
accomplishments.

00:05:43.259 --> 00:05:48.431
But, we also have the ability
to destroy all of those habitats

00:05:48.473 --> 00:05:49.515
and everything in between.

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:54.520
[Jeff] Can looking at evolution

00:05:54.562 --> 00:05:57.815
help us understand the
current conscious self we have,

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:00.318
and the future of humanity?

00:06:07.825 --> 00:06:08.951
From an evolutionary
perspective,

00:06:08.993 --> 00:06:11.371
humans are just another species.

00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:13.206
We're unique, but
evolutionary biology

00:06:13.206 --> 00:06:14.832
sees every species as unique.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.003
[birds chirp]
[owl hoots]

00:06:19.045 --> 00:06:22.715
Every species, everywhere,
is a genius at survival,

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:24.634
otherwise it wouldn't be here.

00:06:25.802 --> 00:06:27.845
Every species can
teach us something.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.391
When you look at all the
different life forms,

00:06:31.391 --> 00:06:32.767
the plants and the animals,

00:06:32.809 --> 00:06:34.685
they just seem so
different to each other.

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:37.021
But, of course, we're all
made of the same stuff.

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:38.731
The atoms, which make
the molecules, 

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:39.982
which produce the DNA,

00:06:40.024 --> 00:06:43.778
and the diversity started this
process of natural selection.

00:06:44.779 --> 00:06:46.239
And, so we know that
natural selection

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:49.575
has allowed diversity to appear
as a process of winning out

00:06:49.617 --> 00:06:52.286
those who are most suited
to the environments.

00:06:52.453 --> 00:06:54.622
Natural selection has evolved

00:06:54.664 --> 00:06:57.208
different lineages of organisms,

00:06:57.250 --> 00:07:00.795
and we are at the top of
one of those lineages.

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:02.255
The fact we're animals

00:07:02.296 --> 00:07:05.758
doesn't mean that underneath
our sophisticated civilization

00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:09.429
we are these utilitarian lumps,
it's exactly the opposite.

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:13.141
What it means is that being
sophisticated and artistic

00:07:13.182 --> 00:07:16.602
goes all the way down
and leads to the level

00:07:16.644 --> 00:07:19.939
of beetles with
iridescent wing cases.

00:07:19.981 --> 00:07:21.774
Because there is something
intrinsically nonutilitarian

00:07:21.816 --> 00:07:24.819
and playful about evolution.

00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:28.364
We're part of the
system, we're nature.

00:07:28.406 --> 00:07:29.949
We're part of the Earth,

00:07:29.991 --> 00:07:35.705
and we're fundamentally
dependent on its good graces.

00:07:38.666 --> 00:07:43.296
Very symbiotic relationship
to everything on this Earth.

00:07:44.964 --> 00:07:47.008
And I understand some
people that if they say

00:07:47.049 --> 00:07:48.426
"no, no, this is 
a science thing,

00:07:48.468 --> 00:07:52.013
"it's not for us, that's
the province of God,

00:07:52.054 --> 00:07:53.681
we shouldn't go there."

00:07:54.015 --> 00:07:56.017
I can hear that view,

00:07:56.058 --> 00:07:59.270
but I really don't think
it's what I see in scripture.

00:07:59.312 --> 00:08:01.314
What I see in
scripture is, c'mon,

00:08:01.355 --> 00:08:02.607
I wanna to show it to you.

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:05.276
I want to reveal myself to you.

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:09.530
I don't see science as
challenging my faith.

00:08:09.572 --> 00:08:11.949
In fact, I see it as
affirming my faith.

00:08:14.452 --> 00:08:16.913
We are new on the planet.

00:08:16.954 --> 00:08:20.500
We are the most newly evolved.

00:08:20.541 --> 00:08:23.044
We're a trial run.

00:08:23.085 --> 00:08:24.378
In terms of evolution,

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:28.508
animals adapt to their
ecological conditions,

00:08:28.508 --> 00:08:30.009
but as humans, we have been able

00:08:30.009 --> 00:08:32.345
to control our 
ecological conditions.

00:08:35.181 --> 00:08:36.849
Evolution itself is a process

00:08:36.891 --> 00:08:38.684
by which organisms
become more efficient

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:40.978
at extracting resources
from their environment.

00:08:42.438 --> 00:08:44.398
Humans are unique in that
what makes us so different

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:47.401
from all other animals is
our mastery of technology.

00:08:47.443 --> 00:08:49.737
We're uniquely able to
transform our environment

00:08:49.779 --> 00:08:51.531
in ways no other animals can.

00:08:54.575 --> 00:08:57.662
[Jeff] We humans are
really big-brained primates

00:08:58.663 --> 00:09:00.540
that lucked out by
becoming bipedal

00:09:00.581 --> 00:09:04.001
and discovering how to coerce
other animals from a distance,

00:09:04.043 --> 00:09:06.045
by being able to throw.

00:09:07.463 --> 00:09:11.926
We figured out how to tame fire
and use it to our advantage.

00:09:12.593 --> 00:09:15.346
Our evolving technology
allowed us to expand

00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:18.307
into new territories and
manipulate the environment

00:09:18.349 --> 00:09:21.060
in ways that gave us an edge.

00:09:26.649 --> 00:09:28.109
Places like this remind me

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:31.070
about how harsh nature can be.

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.030
We're so used to living
in air conditioning,

00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:35.908
and having the comfort
of the modern world,

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:37.535
but when you go out into nature

00:09:37.577 --> 00:09:39.245
and experience it first hand,

00:09:39.287 --> 00:09:41.038
you're reminded very powerfully

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:43.457
about how weak we
are as an animal.

00:09:45.543 --> 00:09:48.254
[Jeff] And this is because
we are fundamentally

00:09:48.296 --> 00:09:49.839
a cultural species.

00:09:51.507 --> 00:09:53.926
Culture is our life
support system.

00:09:55.928 --> 00:09:58.931
Our cumulative culture allows
us to cushion ourselves

00:09:58.973 --> 00:10:01.851
against the harsh realities
of the environment

00:10:01.892 --> 00:10:03.436
and to reshape the environment.

00:10:07.607 --> 00:10:10.943
Our culture is an integral
part of our ecology.

00:10:12.111 --> 00:10:15.906
We can trace human ancestors
back over four million years,

00:10:15.948 --> 00:10:20.453
and anatomically modern humans
for more than 200,000 years

00:10:20.453 --> 00:10:23.873
and we have inherited their
successful survival traits.

00:10:25.082 --> 00:10:27.710
These traits are in all
of us, to some extent,

00:10:27.752 --> 00:10:30.463
traits like optimizing time,

00:10:31.881 --> 00:10:35.259
really caring about what
other people think about us,

00:10:35.301 --> 00:10:37.386
and comparing
ourselves to others.

00:10:40.139 --> 00:10:44.143
We are still trying to attain
the same daily emotional state

00:10:44.185 --> 00:10:46.187
as our successful ancestors.

00:10:49.607 --> 00:10:52.568
We carry the same
neurotransmitters in our brains

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:54.487
that created the
exhilarating feeling

00:10:54.528 --> 00:10:56.614
our ancient ancestors got

00:10:56.656 --> 00:10:58.449
when they had the
unexpected reward

00:10:58.491 --> 00:11:01.952
of finding a berry or a nut,

00:11:01.994 --> 00:11:04.205
or were looking forward
to a successful hunt.

00:11:06.165 --> 00:11:08.709
But in a culture of
amazing technology,

00:11:08.751 --> 00:11:11.587
we are surrounded by
ready-made stimuli

00:11:11.629 --> 00:11:13.964
pushing us to reward ourselves.

00:11:15.383 --> 00:11:18.177
Our evolutionary impulses
can be easily hijacked.

00:11:25.726 --> 00:11:27.645
Irrespective of where
you grew up in the world,

00:11:27.687 --> 00:11:31.023
it's in our DNA to
copy those around us.

00:11:31.065 --> 00:11:33.025
For humans, imitation
is part and parcel

00:11:33.067 --> 00:11:35.027
of what it is to be
a member of a tribe.

00:11:36.987 --> 00:11:38.364
[Jeff] Now our adaptations

00:11:38.406 --> 00:11:40.866
are primarily to the
cultural environment

00:11:40.908 --> 00:11:43.035
we created around us.

00:11:43.077 --> 00:11:45.371
If we don't fit
into our culture,

00:11:45.413 --> 00:11:46.831
we're not going to reproduce,

00:11:46.872 --> 00:11:49.375
we're not going to
survive very well.

00:11:49.375 --> 00:11:51.585
We're going to be ostracized,

00:11:51.627 --> 00:11:55.464
and being ostracized in a
social species like ours,

00:11:55.506 --> 00:11:57.383
can feel like a death sentence.

00:11:59.844 --> 00:12:01.887
Do we have a human
brain mismatched

00:12:01.887 --> 00:12:04.265
between how we
evolved to be here,

00:12:04.306 --> 00:12:08.185
and the modern circumstances
we find ourselves in?

00:12:08.227 --> 00:12:11.731
Are our needs any different
from our ancestors?

00:12:13.232 --> 00:12:16.110
Need, the word, can mean
strongly desire, can't it,

00:12:16.152 --> 00:12:18.529
like, I really need
a bar of chocolate,

00:12:18.571 --> 00:12:21.240
and I'm afraid to say,
that from my point of view,

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:23.075
I'm sure Neanderthals
would have totally loved

00:12:23.075 --> 00:12:24.452
Coca-Cola Zero.

00:12:24.493 --> 00:12:25.953
Like, they looked
really good and pure

00:12:25.995 --> 00:12:27.705
because they didn't
have it, you know.

00:12:27.747 --> 00:12:29.373
It's us poor saps,
but, presumably,

00:12:29.415 --> 00:12:31.333
they got the same
nervous system.

00:12:31.375 --> 00:12:33.961
If it's true that Neanderthals

00:12:34.003 --> 00:12:36.422
would have also become
addicted to Angry Birds,

00:12:37.673 --> 00:12:39.633
there's something true
in contemporary society,

00:12:39.675 --> 00:12:41.719
contemporary society is
saying something true

00:12:41.761 --> 00:12:43.763
about ancient people.

00:12:47.099 --> 00:12:49.560
This is a modern human brain,
and as far as we can tell,

00:12:49.602 --> 00:12:51.604
it's identical to the
brain of individuals

00:12:51.604 --> 00:12:53.689
who lived 50,000 years ago.

00:12:53.731 --> 00:12:55.816
But of course, the
information which is stored

00:12:55.858 --> 00:12:57.276
in the modern brain
is very different

00:12:57.318 --> 00:12:59.069
to that of our ancestors.

00:12:59.111 --> 00:13:01.655
The brain really, basically
encodes information,

00:13:01.697 --> 00:13:02.907
and that information will change

00:13:02.948 --> 00:13:04.784
from one generation to the next.

00:13:04.784 --> 00:13:06.911
So you can see the
course of human evolution

00:13:06.952 --> 00:13:09.955
as the accumulation of
wisdom and knowledge

00:13:09.997 --> 00:13:12.416
that we pass on from one
generation to the next.

00:13:13.959 --> 00:13:16.337
[wind howls]

00:13:22.134 --> 00:13:25.054
Our perceptual system
isn't attuned to

00:13:25.095 --> 00:13:29.767
the massive changes that
are happening on the planet,

00:13:29.809 --> 00:13:33.896
nor are they attuned to the
very fine-grained details

00:13:33.938 --> 00:13:35.231
of those changes.

00:13:35.272 --> 00:13:38.692
Our brain's alarm
system for threat,

00:13:38.734 --> 00:13:40.986
the amygdala, the
emotional centers,

00:13:41.028 --> 00:13:43.906
are very tuned to
these earlier dangers

00:13:43.948 --> 00:13:45.658
from a time when that
rustle in the bushes

00:13:45.658 --> 00:13:48.160
might be a tiger that
was about to eat you,

00:13:49.245 --> 00:13:51.831
or today's symbolic realities,

00:13:51.831 --> 00:13:54.792
I'm not being treated fair,
or "honey, we have to talk",

00:13:54.834 --> 00:13:56.627
or whatever it is
that triggers you.

00:14:00.005 --> 00:14:02.383
We have a very quick system

00:14:02.424 --> 00:14:05.344
that is sort of dealing
with gut reactions

00:14:05.386 --> 00:14:11.225
with reflex responses, with
an instant response to danger.

00:14:12.309 --> 00:14:15.104
And on the other hand,
we have a slower,

00:14:15.145 --> 00:14:19.900
more deliberative system
which takes time to engage,

00:14:19.942 --> 00:14:22.736
and which is slow and ponderous,

00:14:22.778 --> 00:14:24.446
that we should keep engaged

00:14:24.488 --> 00:14:27.867
when we're looking at complex
issues in the world around us.

00:14:29.243 --> 00:14:33.163
[gentle instrumental music]

00:14:36.041 --> 00:14:38.294
[Jeff] We are physical
and biological beings

00:14:38.335 --> 00:14:40.880
living in an ocean
of cosmic energy.

00:14:42.548 --> 00:14:44.758
That sounds pretty
trippy, and it is.

00:14:46.844 --> 00:14:50.389
Where every part contains
information about the whole.

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:58.522
The long view is both
forward and back.

00:14:58.564 --> 00:15:01.108
[birds chirping]

00:15:08.532 --> 00:15:10.492
For 99.9% of our
evolutionary history,

00:15:10.534 --> 00:15:12.244
we were hunting and
gathering people,

00:15:12.286 --> 00:15:14.079
living in small kin groups

00:15:14.121 --> 00:15:15.915
where everybody was
related to everybody

00:15:15.915 --> 00:15:17.750
and therefore we had
a vested interest

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:20.085
in taking care of each other.

00:15:20.085 --> 00:15:23.088
We also lived off of
the land in such a way

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:25.758
that we did not live beyond
the carrying capacity

00:15:25.799 --> 00:15:27.384
of the environment.

00:15:27.426 --> 00:15:31.263
[midtempo instrumental music]

00:15:34.558 --> 00:15:36.936
There was no way to store
food for long periods of time,

00:15:36.936 --> 00:15:40.356
and therefore, there was no
ability to acquire surplus.

00:15:42.775 --> 00:15:43.817
What this resulted in

00:15:43.859 --> 00:15:46.403
was it created a very
egalitarian social system

00:15:46.445 --> 00:15:47.821
where women had autonomy,

00:15:47.863 --> 00:15:50.532
there was no real rigid
dominance hierarchy among men,

00:15:52.159 --> 00:15:54.286
and it was only after the
invention of agriculture

00:15:54.328 --> 00:15:56.246
and the domestication
of plants and animals

00:15:56.288 --> 00:15:57.748
that humans were faced
with the situation

00:15:57.790 --> 00:16:00.125
of what to do with
these surplus resources.

00:16:04.213 --> 00:16:07.257
[Jeff] This was the beginning
of the Neolithic Revolution.

00:16:07.299 --> 00:16:09.426
We started farming
for the first time.

00:16:10.469 --> 00:16:12.262
Everything took off from there,

00:16:12.304 --> 00:16:15.599
and that's only been
around 12,000 years.

00:16:15.641 --> 00:16:18.143
That's a blink of an eye
in evolutionary terms.

00:16:20.646 --> 00:16:22.898
Controlling the production
and distribution

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:25.442
of these surplus
resources led to a change

00:16:25.484 --> 00:16:28.153
in human cultural and
social organization.

00:16:29.488 --> 00:16:31.824
[cannon fire]
[crowd yelling]

00:16:33.575 --> 00:16:36.328
Cultural evolution is
a transferrable system

00:16:36.328 --> 00:16:38.372
a lot like DNA.

00:16:38.414 --> 00:16:41.250
It has all the properties
of an evolving system.

00:16:43.085 --> 00:16:46.046
Since we found agriculture,
things changed.

00:16:46.088 --> 00:16:50.134
And we we started to
act like a larger entity

00:16:50.175 --> 00:16:51.885
just like an ant colony,

00:16:51.927 --> 00:16:54.638
or a termite colony,
or a beehive.

00:16:57.683 --> 00:17:00.019
[bees buzz]

00:17:03.063 --> 00:17:05.858
[Jeff] Bees and ants
are individual organisms

00:17:05.899 --> 00:17:07.693
performing different roles.

00:17:10.362 --> 00:17:13.741
They rush about to feed
or defend the colony

00:17:14.408 --> 00:17:17.286
and collectively they
create a superorganism.

00:17:18.787 --> 00:17:21.874
Of course, humans are
not social insects,

00:17:21.874 --> 00:17:24.418
but if you were able to
watch a village or a city

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:27.046
from a distance over time,

00:17:27.087 --> 00:17:31.008
it looks a lot like a growing,
interdependent superorganism.

00:17:33.052 --> 00:17:36.180
A reorganization of
material existence

00:17:36.221 --> 00:17:37.765
with far reaching implications.

00:17:39.808 --> 00:17:42.478
[traffic hums]
[siren wails]

00:17:42.519 --> 00:17:45.022
Today we live in a world
of material abundance

00:17:45.064 --> 00:17:48.108
and inexpensive energy,
and this leads us to think

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:50.027
that this is normal for humans,

00:17:50.069 --> 00:17:51.904
that it's the normal
human condition.

00:17:53.572 --> 00:17:57.076
Early societies, even
through the Middle Ages,

00:17:57.076 --> 00:17:59.078
and up until almost
the modern era

00:18:00.162 --> 00:18:01.747
had 90% of their economies

00:18:01.747 --> 00:18:03.624
devoted to the
production of energy,

00:18:03.665 --> 00:18:06.001
primarily in the form of food.

00:18:06.043 --> 00:18:07.836
That meant that 90%
of what people did

00:18:07.878 --> 00:18:11.090
involved producing energy, in
other words, just getting by.

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:18.931
[Jeff] Our lives were
based on solar flows.

00:18:18.931 --> 00:18:20.599
Sunlight hit the earth,

00:18:20.599 --> 00:18:23.477
photosynthesis grew the plants,

00:18:23.519 --> 00:18:26.772
rain and soil, with the
nutrients, grew crops,

00:18:26.814 --> 00:18:30.484
animals ate the crops,
and we ate the animals.

00:18:32.611 --> 00:18:35.656
Energy transforms from
one state to another.

00:18:38.075 --> 00:18:41.578
Our bodies were product of the
current sunlight of the day.

00:18:45.958 --> 00:18:49.795
And we are just transient
entities, which appear,

00:18:49.837 --> 00:18:52.506
move a little bit,
and then disappear.

00:18:52.548 --> 00:18:55.217
That's the way the system works.

00:18:55.259 --> 00:18:58.887
And, we have this
chance to be alive,

00:18:59.847 --> 00:19:02.391
to move on this planet, to
do things on this planet,

00:19:02.432 --> 00:19:04.226
to create things on this planet

00:19:04.268 --> 00:19:06.311
because we have this
gigantic flux of energy

00:19:06.311 --> 00:19:07.604
coming from the sun.

00:19:08.981 --> 00:19:12.651
[midtempo electronic music]

00:19:14.528 --> 00:19:17.364
The whole universe
is a giant machine

00:19:17.406 --> 00:19:19.992
for dispersing
energy potentials.

00:19:21.118 --> 00:19:23.036
Energy is everything
in the sense

00:19:23.078 --> 00:19:26.039
that nothing moves in
the universe without

00:19:26.081 --> 00:19:28.750
an energy potential available.

00:19:34.298 --> 00:19:36.800
[birds chirp]

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:40.053
We have been also
able to increase

00:19:40.095 --> 00:19:43.140
this already very
large amount of energy

00:19:43.182 --> 00:19:46.518
by means of using
ancient processes

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:51.899
frozen below the ground
in the Earth's crust

00:19:52.566 --> 00:19:54.401
in the form of what
we call fossil fuels.

00:19:57.070 --> 00:19:59.156
[Jeff] Fossil sunlight
is so powerful

00:19:59.198 --> 00:20:01.575
it's indistinguishable
from magic.

00:20:02.868 --> 00:20:05.037
And we're mining
this ancient sunlight

00:20:05.078 --> 00:20:07.706
in a very brief period
of human history.

00:20:09.082 --> 00:20:12.711
[ambient electronic music]

00:20:16.089 --> 00:20:19.885
A chemical composition
of 50% of the protein

00:20:19.885 --> 00:20:23.555
in our bodies and 80% of
the nitrogen in our bodies

00:20:23.597 --> 00:20:26.725
indirectly comes from
the chemical signature

00:20:26.725 --> 00:20:30.145
of this fossil sunlight
that we're mining.

00:20:31.063 --> 00:20:34.358
So, we are different
than our ancestors.

00:20:34.399 --> 00:20:37.861
They were made of sunlight,
we are made of fossil fuels.

00:20:37.903 --> 00:20:39.571
In hunting and
gathering societies,

00:20:39.613 --> 00:20:42.658
solar energy can
provide enough food

00:20:42.699 --> 00:20:46.078
to support maybe one
person per square mile.

00:20:46.078 --> 00:20:49.748
Basic subsistence agriculture
can support more people,

00:20:49.790 --> 00:20:51.416
but nowhere near the
population densities

00:20:51.416 --> 00:20:53.460
that we have today.

00:20:53.502 --> 00:20:55.003
The way we live is an anomaly.

00:21:02.177 --> 00:21:05.514
So there's really no
way for anyone to know

00:21:05.555 --> 00:21:06.932
at a sort of visceral level

00:21:06.932 --> 00:21:08.517
that there are any
problems with the system,

00:21:08.558 --> 00:21:10.894
and if you look
around, it all works.

00:21:14.523 --> 00:21:15.941
We know historically, I mean,

00:21:15.983 --> 00:21:17.609
to the extent that we
even pay attention to it,

00:21:17.609 --> 00:21:19.111
we used to use wood.

00:21:19.152 --> 00:21:21.947
We're sort of aware that
our predecessors used wood

00:21:21.947 --> 00:21:23.240
and they used so much of it,

00:21:23.282 --> 00:21:24.783
they had to switch
to something else.

00:21:25.325 --> 00:21:26.702
They switched to coal.

00:21:30.872 --> 00:21:32.874
And then they switched to oil.

00:21:32.916 --> 00:21:34.209
80 years ago,

00:21:34.251 --> 00:21:37.879
the oil in America was
just under the surface.

00:21:37.921 --> 00:21:39.506
We would need very
little machinery

00:21:39.548 --> 00:21:40.966
to get these gusher wells,

00:21:40.966 --> 00:21:44.803
and we would get out
over 100 times the energy

00:21:44.845 --> 00:21:45.679
that we put in.

00:21:51.518 --> 00:21:54.604
Suddenly this whole new world
of transportation opened up,

00:21:54.646 --> 00:21:56.690
and with that this
entirely new model

00:21:56.732 --> 00:21:57.983
for how we fed ourselves,

00:21:57.983 --> 00:22:00.152
how we clothed ourselves,
how we built shelter,

00:22:00.193 --> 00:22:03.030
and it totally changed
the way we live.

00:22:04.656 --> 00:22:07.993
It made it possible for us to
move away from the country.

00:22:08.035 --> 00:22:10.537
[birds chirp]

00:22:11.997 --> 00:22:14.666
Throughout human history, we
had to live sort of scattered

00:22:14.666 --> 00:22:18.712
in order to not tax any
particular resource too greatly.

00:22:20.797 --> 00:22:24.259
As we began to be able to
import food, or import energy,

00:22:24.301 --> 00:22:26.136
we could pretty much live
wherever we wanted to,

00:22:26.178 --> 00:22:28.096
and where we wanted to
live were in the cities.

00:22:28.138 --> 00:22:29.806
So, we have this
massive migration,

00:22:29.848 --> 00:22:31.350
and we empty the countryside.

00:22:34.853 --> 00:22:36.104
We all move to the city

00:22:36.146 --> 00:22:37.522
because we're growing
food in the country,

00:22:37.522 --> 00:22:39.816
and we can just
transport it to the city,

00:22:39.858 --> 00:22:40.817
and once you're in the city,

00:22:40.859 --> 00:22:42.194
then you can do all
these other things.

00:22:42.903 --> 00:22:44.196
You know, you can create
all this knowledge,

00:22:44.237 --> 00:22:45.655
you can create technologies,

00:22:45.697 --> 00:22:48.158
you can create this
massive economic growth,

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:49.826
and the longer you've
been living that way,

00:22:49.868 --> 00:22:51.578
the harder it is to remember

00:22:51.620 --> 00:22:53.580
what allowed it to start
in the first place,

00:22:53.622 --> 00:22:56.083
and that was this
idea of cheap energy.

00:22:57.376 --> 00:23:00.962
[ambient electronic music]

00:23:05.384 --> 00:23:08.720
[moves into uptempo
electronic music]

00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:12.849
Humans gradually became a
functional superorganism.

00:23:12.891 --> 00:23:16.895
At first, we would
maximize grain surplus.

00:23:18.397 --> 00:23:20.899
We would grow more
grain and store it,

00:23:20.941 --> 00:23:23.235
and that would allow our
population to increase

00:23:23.276 --> 00:23:25.070
and our territory to expand,

00:23:26.071 --> 00:23:28.907
but now we're maximizing
surplus value.

00:23:30.242 --> 00:23:32.953
We're maximizing financial,
digital, electronic

00:23:32.994 --> 00:23:35.705
representations of surplus.

00:23:37.207 --> 00:23:39.918
But all money really
effectively is

00:23:39.918 --> 00:23:42.587
is a claim on some
energy services.

00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:50.303
And so what ends up happening,
is money, trade, technology

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:53.682
are all in service
of the superorganism.

00:24:00.397 --> 00:24:02.482
[Jeff] It's nearly
impossible for individuals

00:24:02.524 --> 00:24:06.278
to function independently
in modern society.

00:24:06.319 --> 00:24:09.614
We contribute to and rely
on a growing superorganism

00:24:09.656 --> 00:24:12.451
to feed us, clothe us,

00:24:12.451 --> 00:24:16.204
power our sophisticated
communications,

00:24:16.246 --> 00:24:18.915
even protect our
societies from each other

00:24:18.957 --> 00:24:21.084
through highly
organized warfare.

00:24:23.712 --> 00:24:28.091
We live within a mesh of
energy-eating interdependence

00:24:28.133 --> 00:24:30.177
and we never really
see the big picture.

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:34.806
A state of plenty can have
unintended consequences.

00:24:36.016 --> 00:24:38.852
It is the paradox of our times.

00:24:39.811 --> 00:24:42.230
[chicks peep]

00:24:43.565 --> 00:24:46.443
Running in the background
of all that we do

00:24:46.485 --> 00:24:49.029
whether we consider ourselves
to be capitalist or soviet

00:24:49.070 --> 00:24:52.991
or feudal, everything
is a kind of program,

00:24:53.033 --> 00:24:55.452
a kind of recipe, a
kind of algorithm,

00:24:55.494 --> 00:25:00.040
the logistical functioning
of Neolithic society,

00:25:00.081 --> 00:25:02.334
which started all
over the world.

00:25:02.375 --> 00:25:05.086
The trouble is that when
you wash, rinse, repeat,

00:25:05.128 --> 00:25:06.838
eventually, when you
do it enough times,

00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:08.757
it starts to reveal its flaws.

00:25:08.798 --> 00:25:12.802
For example, me starting my car
is statistically meaningless

00:25:12.844 --> 00:25:14.638
from a global warming
point of view.

00:25:14.679 --> 00:25:17.432
Billions and billions
of car ignition turnings

00:25:17.474 --> 00:25:19.017
has a meaning, and
that's the paradox.

00:25:19.059 --> 00:25:20.310
All of a sudden,

00:25:20.352 --> 00:25:22.854
we realize that we're
part of a superorganism.

00:25:22.854 --> 00:25:25.774
We are enmeshed in
systems of production,

00:25:25.815 --> 00:25:29.194
systems of manufacture,
systems of use.

00:25:31.196 --> 00:25:33.365
It's incredibly
important to recognize

00:25:33.406 --> 00:25:38.036
that we're all
part of the system.

00:25:38.078 --> 00:25:41.581
[ambient electronic music]

00:25:46.503 --> 00:25:49.381
As social organisms,
we're something like ants.

00:25:50.257 --> 00:25:52.050
Our signals that we exchange
are far more complicated

00:25:52.050 --> 00:25:55.845
than the simple chemical
signals that ants exchange,

00:25:55.887 --> 00:25:58.932
but we do exchange
signals, and as humans,

00:25:58.974 --> 00:26:01.476
we take individual actions
that, on the whole,

00:26:01.518 --> 00:26:03.228
cause emergent behavior.

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:04.688
The problem that's
happening today

00:26:04.729 --> 00:26:07.440
is that the emergent behavior
is growing very complex

00:26:07.482 --> 00:26:10.110
and detrimental to the
planet, to ourselves,

00:26:10.151 --> 00:26:11.444
and to many other species.

00:26:11.945 --> 00:26:14.990
[geese call]

00:26:15.031 --> 00:26:17.867
[Jeff] Emergent
behavior is all around us,

00:26:17.909 --> 00:26:20.245
and can be fastmoving
and unpredictable.

00:26:21.121 --> 00:26:23.415
No one could foresee our
patterns of interaction

00:26:23.456 --> 00:26:27.419
would lead to the
internet, the smart phone,

00:26:27.419 --> 00:26:29.546
the disappearance
of phone booths,

00:26:29.588 --> 00:26:31.756
and the rise of server farms.

00:26:32.716 --> 00:26:34.593
Who knows what will happen next?

00:26:36.886 --> 00:26:38.597
[birds chirp]
[camera shutter clicks]

00:26:38.638 --> 00:26:40.890
We're all in this together.

00:26:40.932 --> 00:26:42.267
Maybe if you're off the grid

00:26:42.267 --> 00:26:44.352
and truly walking
everywhere you go,

00:26:44.394 --> 00:26:47.939
maybe then you have the
right to polish your halo.

00:26:47.939 --> 00:26:50.275
We're tiny little microbes

00:26:50.317 --> 00:26:52.444
on the surface of
this huge planet.

00:26:52.485 --> 00:26:55.780
We can actually affect the
planet and cause it to respond.

00:26:56.948 --> 00:27:00.452
[somber electronic music]

00:27:09.419 --> 00:27:14.924
If we don't stop emitting
carbon dioxide at these rates,

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:17.802
pretty soon we're
going to be in trouble.

00:27:17.802 --> 00:27:20.972
We're going to have massive
climatic disruption.

00:27:20.972 --> 00:27:23.099
The rainfall belts will move.

00:27:24.184 --> 00:27:27.062
People in the
hundreds of millions,

00:27:27.103 --> 00:27:28.897
maybe a billion
and a half people,

00:27:29.356 --> 00:27:33.360
will have problems getting
access to fresh water and food.

00:27:35.528 --> 00:27:37.739
The issue is not what
the world is like

00:27:37.781 --> 00:27:40.825
when it's cold or
when it's warm.

00:27:40.825 --> 00:27:43.286
We are in a time of change.

00:27:43.328 --> 00:27:46.331
What is interesting is
how the world changes

00:27:46.331 --> 00:27:49.000
when you dramatically,
in our case,

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:51.211
increase the concentration
of carbon dioxide

00:27:51.252 --> 00:27:53.171
and other greenhouse
gases in the atmosphere.

00:27:53.171 --> 00:27:55.882
Or, when it suffers
mammoth biodiversity loss

00:27:55.924 --> 00:27:57.342
caused by our own behavior.

00:27:59.344 --> 00:28:00.929
If there's a big change coming,

00:28:00.970 --> 00:28:02.889
and we want to know what
it might do in the future,

00:28:02.931 --> 00:28:04.099
what do we do?

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:05.850
We look at what's
happened in the past.

00:28:05.892 --> 00:28:08.937
Geological history largely
reinforces the lessons

00:28:08.978 --> 00:28:10.355
from the climate models.

00:28:10.355 --> 00:28:12.190
When they say the
doubling of carbon dioxide

00:28:12.232 --> 00:28:13.650
will lead to about 2 1/2 degrees

00:28:13.692 --> 00:28:15.110
to three degrees of warming,

00:28:15.151 --> 00:28:18.029
the history of the Earth is
broadly in agreement with that.

00:28:19.364 --> 00:28:20.907
So a lot of my own
research is focused on

00:28:20.949 --> 00:28:22.867
the reconstruction
of past climate.

00:28:25.745 --> 00:28:27.789
We look at dramatic transitions

00:28:29.290 --> 00:28:32.961
and there's not a lot of those,
because in Earth history,

00:28:33.002 --> 00:28:35.004
things change gradually.

00:28:36.131 --> 00:28:37.549
Three years ago I
started on a project.

00:28:37.590 --> 00:28:38.842
It was to look at an event

00:28:38.883 --> 00:28:40.385
that was about 120
million years ago.

00:28:40.427 --> 00:28:42.721
And it was to
explore exactly this.

00:28:42.762 --> 00:28:44.305
It was what we
generally considered

00:28:44.347 --> 00:28:48.727
a rapid ocean acidification
event, a rapid warming event,

00:28:48.768 --> 00:28:51.604
and we thought, let's look at
this, and see what happened.

00:28:51.646 --> 00:28:53.898
And as we studied
it more and more,

00:28:53.940 --> 00:28:56.526
and we got better and
better at age models,

00:28:56.568 --> 00:28:58.611
we got better and
better reconstructions

00:28:58.653 --> 00:29:01.072
of the climate change,
it became apparent

00:29:01.072 --> 00:29:02.282
that this particular event

00:29:02.323 --> 00:29:04.075
that happened about
100 million years ago,

00:29:04.075 --> 00:29:09.080
the climate change happened
over about 40,000 years.

00:29:09.789 --> 00:29:11.666
This is the same degree
of climate change

00:29:11.708 --> 00:29:14.836
that we think might happen
over the next 100 years.

00:29:20.216 --> 00:29:22.927
There's much more
energy in the system.

00:29:22.927 --> 00:29:25.555
[waves crash]

00:29:25.597 --> 00:29:28.600
We have very little knowledge

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:33.438
of how Earth's biological
life support systems

00:29:33.438 --> 00:29:36.191
will respond to dramatic
and rapid change.

00:29:40.779 --> 00:29:44.783
We have extraordinary
biomes on the planet

00:29:47.452 --> 00:29:51.539
that all are individual
pieces of a jigsaw.

00:29:51.581 --> 00:29:54.167
[animals call]

00:29:56.795 --> 00:29:58.129
The picture is clear

00:29:58.129 --> 00:30:00.632
when they're all
functional and all present.

00:30:00.673 --> 00:30:02.634
But start picking
those pieces off,

00:30:02.675 --> 00:30:05.303
and you can no
longer see, really,

00:30:05.303 --> 00:30:06.930
what that picture looks like,

00:30:06.971 --> 00:30:11.351
and the functionality of the
entire system is out of whack.

00:30:12.477 --> 00:30:14.938
[bee buzzes]

00:30:17.106 --> 00:30:18.733
The arctic icecap is shrinking,

00:30:19.984 --> 00:30:21.736
the Greenland ice
mass is being lost

00:30:21.778 --> 00:30:23.655
at 300 gigatons a year,

00:30:23.696 --> 00:30:26.825
that's 300 cubic kilometers
of ice a year is disappearing.

00:30:27.992 --> 00:30:30.870
The ocean is changing,
sea level rise,

00:30:30.912 --> 00:30:32.789
we're seeing significant
sea level rise,

00:30:32.831 --> 00:30:36.167
we're expecting a couple
of feet, maybe more

00:30:36.167 --> 00:30:38.044
before the end of the century.

00:30:38.086 --> 00:30:39.712
So all these things are facts.

00:30:39.754 --> 00:30:40.839
[gulls call]

00:30:40.880 --> 00:30:43.174
It's not so much
the change itself,

00:30:43.174 --> 00:30:45.009
but it's how fast
it's happening.

00:30:45.051 --> 00:30:49.055
The faster the change comes,
the less time we have to adapt.

00:30:49.097 --> 00:30:50.473
The faster the change comes,

00:30:50.515 --> 00:30:53.476
the less time the
trees have to adapt,

00:30:53.518 --> 00:30:55.353
or plankton living in the ocean.

00:30:56.229 --> 00:30:57.856
The faster the change comes,

00:30:57.897 --> 00:31:00.942
the less time ecosystems
have to adapt.

00:31:01.651 --> 00:31:03.069
And the more complex these are,

00:31:03.111 --> 00:31:04.696
the harder it is
to actually predict

00:31:04.696 --> 00:31:07.198
what the consequences
of that change will be.

00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:13.079
Climate change is going to
put huge stress on societies

00:31:13.121 --> 00:31:17.876
that are already under
internal political stress,

00:31:17.876 --> 00:31:21.838
and stress induced
by lack of resources.

00:31:21.963 --> 00:31:24.591
Like, food availability,
water availability,

00:31:24.632 --> 00:31:27.552
dramatic events associated
with climate change,

00:31:27.552 --> 00:31:30.889
all of which potentially
will exacerbate the divisions

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:32.181
in society.

00:31:32.223 --> 00:31:34.809
Access to wealth
creates divisions.

00:31:34.851 --> 00:31:38.688
Access to food and water makes
divisions very pronounced.

00:31:41.024 --> 00:31:44.360
It's gonna impact the way
humans live on the planet.

00:31:44.402 --> 00:31:49.032
And when that impacts, then
people move, people are angry,

00:31:49.073 --> 00:31:52.118
land becomes less
valuable, or more valuable.

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:56.289
Populations shift, and those
shifts destabilize governments.

00:31:56.331 --> 00:31:58.333
Huh!
[weapon fires]

00:32:05.632 --> 00:32:07.425
[Jeff] We might look up at night

00:32:07.467 --> 00:32:09.469
and wonder at the far away stars

00:32:09.510 --> 00:32:12.430
as if we were in a pristine
crystal snow globe.

00:32:16.559 --> 00:32:19.145
But our problems can
no longer be pushed

00:32:19.187 --> 00:32:21.272
into the back yard
of the less powerful,

00:32:21.272 --> 00:32:23.441
the less fortunate among us.

00:32:24.651 --> 00:32:29.030
No, here we are, and
here we were made,

00:32:29.072 --> 00:32:32.951
where everything from
farmers, dogs, LED lights,

00:32:32.951 --> 00:32:38.039
flowers, pencils, politicians,
microwaves, and penguins,

00:32:38.122 --> 00:32:42.877
the past, present, and
future are all interrelated.

00:32:43.795 --> 00:32:47.465
[gentle instrumental music]

00:32:51.928 --> 00:32:54.430
[birds chirp]

00:32:55.723 --> 00:32:56.849
[machine buzzes]

00:32:56.891 --> 00:32:58.351
In the next 10 years,

00:32:58.393 --> 00:33:02.480
we shall increase
our wealth by 50%.

00:33:02.522 --> 00:33:04.649
The profound question is

00:33:04.691 --> 00:33:09.696
does this mean we will be
50% richer, in a real sense,

00:33:10.571 --> 00:33:14.826
50% better off, 50% happier?

00:33:14.993 --> 00:33:17.328
And so to just get more facts

00:33:17.370 --> 00:33:20.081
about climate change
or about oil depletion

00:33:20.123 --> 00:33:22.417
or about environmental
destruction,

00:33:22.458 --> 00:33:24.335
people don't know what to do

00:33:24.377 --> 00:33:27.171
because they're part
of this organism

00:33:27.213 --> 00:33:31.134
trying to get more
feelgood brain chemicals.

00:33:32.135 --> 00:33:33.594
The treadmill is getting
smaller, and smaller,

00:33:33.636 --> 00:33:35.179
and smaller, and smaller.

00:33:35.221 --> 00:33:37.473
And, the number of people who
want to be on the treadmill

00:33:37.515 --> 00:33:40.351
is getting bigger and bigger
and bigger and bigger.

00:33:40.393 --> 00:33:42.395
And eventually, there's
going to be no room to run,

00:33:42.437 --> 00:33:44.022
and so, people will fall off.

00:33:45.273 --> 00:33:47.734
Many people will fall off.

00:33:52.697 --> 00:33:57.493
We are profoundly motivated
by having purpose.

00:33:57.535 --> 00:34:01.330
We're motivated by having
a sense of autonomy,

00:34:01.372 --> 00:34:02.874
efficacy, and control.

00:34:03.666 --> 00:34:08.588
Where it seems that our gifts,
our energy, our life force

00:34:08.629 --> 00:34:10.965
is not going to
have some impact,

00:34:11.007 --> 00:34:13.259
we will simply withdraw,

00:34:13.301 --> 00:34:18.139
and we will very likely
redirect that energy

00:34:18.181 --> 00:34:22.518
into where we can feel
we have most impact.

00:34:22.560 --> 00:34:25.396
And where is that in
our culture right now?

00:34:25.396 --> 00:34:27.482
It's in consumption.

00:34:27.523 --> 00:34:31.402
The economist Immanuel
Wallerstein has talked about

00:34:31.402 --> 00:34:34.405
world systems theory, in
which all things are connected

00:34:34.405 --> 00:34:37.075
for better, and often for worse,

00:34:37.075 --> 00:34:39.952
so, living here in the
most industrialized part

00:34:39.994 --> 00:34:43.414
of the industrialized world,
our tentacles reach everywhere.

00:34:43.456 --> 00:34:46.709
[wind whistles]

00:34:46.751 --> 00:34:49.253
[birds chirp]

00:34:51.130 --> 00:34:53.591
[Jeff] Efficacy is
the ability to produce

00:34:53.633 --> 00:34:55.635
a desired result.

00:34:55.676 --> 00:34:59.222
But are the results we're
achieving, the ones we intend?

00:35:00.306 --> 00:35:02.642
Wild animals are
disappearing forever.

00:35:03.935 --> 00:35:07.688
Our intelligence is remaking
the world before our eyes.

00:35:08.981 --> 00:35:11.275
Today we can just click
on a link somewhere,

00:35:11.275 --> 00:35:13.444
and have something
appear on our doorstep

00:35:13.486 --> 00:35:16.697
without having any idea of what
went into getting it there.

00:35:18.574 --> 00:35:21.119
All we know about what
got it there is the price.

00:35:21.160 --> 00:35:23.704
We get more and more
detached from the actual cost

00:35:23.746 --> 00:35:27.125
of creating and transporting
what we're using.

00:35:33.422 --> 00:35:35.133
Energy is really at
the center of physics,

00:35:35.133 --> 00:35:36.634
but for the lay person,

00:35:36.634 --> 00:35:38.177
probably the easiest
way to explain it

00:35:38.219 --> 00:35:40.429
is that energy is the
ability to do work.

00:35:42.974 --> 00:35:44.892
Work is force through distance.

00:35:45.726 --> 00:35:49.355
So every time that you move
something from China to the US

00:35:49.397 --> 00:35:52.150
or from the floor to the
ceiling, that takes energy.

00:35:52.191 --> 00:35:54.819
When you drive to work,
obviously it takes energy.

00:35:54.861 --> 00:35:56.320
To boil one quart of water,

00:35:56.362 --> 00:35:59.657
it takes up the equivalent
of 500 calories of food.

00:35:59.657 --> 00:36:02.368
Any kind of transformation
requires energy.

00:36:02.410 --> 00:36:04.328
We are powerful
creatures on this planet,

00:36:04.370 --> 00:36:06.789
but we still need the
ecosystem to survive,

00:36:06.831 --> 00:36:09.292
so we don't want to
destroy the ecosystem

00:36:09.333 --> 00:36:11.502
because it would be rather bad.

00:36:11.544 --> 00:36:14.797
And this is a transition, you
can call it also a revolution,

00:36:14.839 --> 00:36:17.175
you can call it
whatever you'd like.

00:36:17.175 --> 00:36:20.344
The point is that there
have been such revolutions,

00:36:20.386 --> 00:36:22.180
such transitions in the past,

00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:25.474
but these transitions
have a cost.

00:36:25.516 --> 00:36:29.061
An energy transition
must be paid in energy.

00:36:29.937 --> 00:36:33.566
[midtempo electronic music]

00:36:35.484 --> 00:36:37.737
[Jeff] Energy is the
currency of life.

00:36:38.696 --> 00:36:41.616
[birds chirp]

00:36:41.657 --> 00:36:44.035
Energy creates
movement and food.

00:36:48.873 --> 00:36:51.125
All life taps energy flows.

00:36:58.925 --> 00:37:01.469
[horse sighs]

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:04.555
We used to measure units of
energy in terms of horsepower.

00:37:08.059 --> 00:37:10.144
One horse could do
the work of 10 men.

00:37:16.150 --> 00:37:19.362
A small tractor could do
the work of 40 horses.

00:37:22.448 --> 00:37:26.369
It would take 450 horses or
more to get the same work done

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:28.746
as just one semi truck.

00:37:29.914 --> 00:37:33.417
And unlike horses, trucks
can work day and night

00:37:33.459 --> 00:37:34.377
in any weather.

00:37:35.795 --> 00:37:39.257
The flow of fossil fuel has
turbocharged our society.

00:37:41.759 --> 00:37:46.764
And so, to just say,
we have the science now

00:37:48.224 --> 00:37:50.977
to realize that these fossil
slaves, they don't complain,

00:37:51.018 --> 00:37:54.313
they don't sleep, they're
tireless, they're super strong,

00:37:54.355 --> 00:37:56.357
but they poop and they breathe,

00:37:56.399 --> 00:37:59.819
and their breath is causing
our biosphere to warm up,

00:37:59.860 --> 00:38:01.529
and our oceans to acidify.

00:38:01.570 --> 00:38:03.155
Let's keep them in the ground.

00:38:03.197 --> 00:38:04.573
It's not quite so simple.

00:38:05.992 --> 00:38:09.495
If you consider the power
that's in fossil fuels,

00:38:09.537 --> 00:38:13.165
90% of the work done in
human economies is done

00:38:13.207 --> 00:38:14.458
by fossil slaves.

00:38:17.295 --> 00:38:18.879
The average American consumes

00:38:18.921 --> 00:38:23.634
220,000 kilocalories, every day.

00:38:23.634 --> 00:38:25.136
We don't think about that,

00:38:25.177 --> 00:38:29.807
we only think about the 3,000
or 3,500 of food that we eat.

00:38:33.227 --> 00:38:34.478
But our energy footprint

00:38:34.478 --> 00:38:36.147
is almost a hundred
times more than that

00:38:36.188 --> 00:38:39.775
if you consider our
buses, and our airplanes,

00:38:39.817 --> 00:38:42.820
and all the hospitals, and the
Disneylands, and the NASCARs,

00:38:42.862 --> 00:38:45.072
and in all the various
things that we buy

00:38:45.114 --> 00:38:46.866
that are imported all
around the world.

00:38:48.993 --> 00:38:52.830
It's what people invest in,
and a lot of pension funds,

00:38:52.872 --> 00:38:56.459
and a lot of the institutions
that humanity depends on,

00:38:56.500 --> 00:38:59.170
of course they
gravitate toward oil.

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:01.172
Renewable energy,
although it's mature

00:39:01.172 --> 00:39:02.757
and it's getting very cheap,

00:39:02.798 --> 00:39:06.510
it's not going to replace
this infrastructure

00:39:06.510 --> 00:39:09.555
and this civilization.

00:39:09.597 --> 00:39:13.017
We have a very limited
amount of time to transition

00:39:13.017 --> 00:39:16.771
to a low-carbon economy, but
it's very naive to just say,

00:39:16.812 --> 00:39:18.022
let's keep it in the ground

00:39:18.064 --> 00:39:19.899
and keep everything
else running.

00:39:19.940 --> 00:39:24.945
So if you ask "can solar
energy power our society

00:39:24.987 --> 00:39:27.823
"as it is today?", then you
already have the answer.

00:39:27.990 --> 00:39:30.493
It is no, it is not possible.

00:39:30.534 --> 00:39:34.789
If you want to switch
from oil and gas and coal

00:39:34.830 --> 00:39:36.999
and to move to solar energy,

00:39:37.041 --> 00:39:39.460
then you have to
change a lot of things.

00:39:39.502 --> 00:39:42.505
Our food system right
now is an energy sink.

00:39:42.546 --> 00:39:47.551
We use 10 to 12 fossil-calories
to produce one food-calorie.

00:39:48.386 --> 00:39:52.181
Right now we have around $100
trillion worth of machinery

00:39:52.223 --> 00:39:55.893
on the planet that uses
gasoline or diesel fuel.

00:39:57.311 --> 00:40:01.565
So, for one barrel of
oil, which we pay $50 for,

00:40:01.565 --> 00:40:06.570
we have thousands of fossil
slaves standing behind us.

00:40:07.696 --> 00:40:10.866
The American inventory
of automobiles,

00:40:10.908 --> 00:40:15.079
250, 270 million cars and
light trucks on the road,

00:40:15.121 --> 00:40:19.917
you're talking about more
than a decade of production

00:40:19.959 --> 00:40:21.794
and you're talking
about more wealth

00:40:21.836 --> 00:40:25.089
than the gross domestic
product in a year to replace it

00:40:25.131 --> 00:40:29.093
So you're not going to make
these changes overnight.

00:40:29.135 --> 00:40:32.263
You see, there is a
parameter which is very basic

00:40:32.263 --> 00:40:35.391
in evaluating these
kinds of things,

00:40:35.433 --> 00:40:39.019
which is called energy
return on energy invested.

00:40:39.061 --> 00:40:41.397
Energy doesn't cost dollars.

00:40:41.439 --> 00:40:44.442
I mean, it does, but
it really costs energy.

00:40:44.483 --> 00:40:46.777
Oil's the biggest
industry on the planet,

00:40:46.819 --> 00:40:50.114
so, it sets the pace,
in terms of inflation.

00:40:50.156 --> 00:40:52.533
So if you raise
the price of oil,

00:40:52.575 --> 00:40:55.953
all the other prices go up,
until people have accommodated,

00:40:55.995 --> 00:40:58.831
and they can still afford
to pay the extraction cost

00:40:58.873 --> 00:40:59.707
for oil.

00:41:00.833 --> 00:41:03.586
So, it's not like we're
running out of oil,

00:41:05.045 --> 00:41:07.465
or coal or gas, it's that
it's getting more costly,

00:41:07.465 --> 00:41:09.508
in energy terms, to get it.

00:41:09.550 --> 00:41:10.801
Because it's more costly,

00:41:10.801 --> 00:41:14.805
it has less benefits
to the rest of society.

00:41:14.805 --> 00:41:17.016
The 1930s, the
Beverly Hillbilly oil

00:41:17.057 --> 00:41:20.019
just bubbling under the
ground, we've used all that.

00:41:23.481 --> 00:41:25.816
Net energy, which
is the energy left

00:41:25.858 --> 00:41:27.359
after we've paid the energy cost

00:41:27.401 --> 00:41:32.364
of finding and extracting it,
net energy is declining now.

00:41:33.616 --> 00:41:37.244
We don't realize the
scale and the stakes

00:41:37.286 --> 00:41:38.287
of what's happening.

00:41:39.371 --> 00:41:41.916
When we take on debt, we
are promising to repay it

00:41:41.957 --> 00:41:43.667
with future energy.

00:41:44.835 --> 00:41:46.921
The fossil fuels will
not last forever,

00:41:46.962 --> 00:41:48.672
they cannot last forever.

00:41:48.672 --> 00:41:52.384
The energy potential
in one barrel of oil

00:41:52.426 --> 00:41:55.554
is equal to a human being
working 40 hours a week

00:41:55.596 --> 00:41:57.848
for 4 1/2 years.

00:42:00.017 --> 00:42:01.769
You have to think to the future,

00:42:01.810 --> 00:42:04.021
you have to save
something for the future

00:42:05.231 --> 00:42:09.193
in order to have a harvest
of renewable energy.

00:42:09.235 --> 00:42:13.614
Do not eat your seed
corn, is an old saying.

00:42:13.656 --> 00:42:17.326
[somber instrumental music]

00:42:21.288 --> 00:42:24.500
[Jeff] Wise farmers know that
if they eat their seed corn,

00:42:24.542 --> 00:42:27.545
there will be nothing left to
plant for a future harvest.

00:42:28.837 --> 00:42:31.382
Will future generations
look back at today

00:42:31.382 --> 00:42:33.425
and view much of the
energy we're burning

00:42:33.467 --> 00:42:34.718
to have been wasted?

00:42:36.387 --> 00:42:38.597
Are we eating our seed corn?

00:42:43.185 --> 00:42:46.313
So many of us have come to
expect the level of comfort

00:42:46.355 --> 00:42:50.109
and convenience unprecedented
in our biological past.

00:42:54.989 --> 00:42:57.866
We need to redefine
our expectations

00:42:57.908 --> 00:43:02.162
not as what we will lose,
but what we might gain,

00:43:02.204 --> 00:43:04.081
by preparing for
something different.

00:43:09.628 --> 00:43:11.255
Oh, fossil fuels are bad?

00:43:11.297 --> 00:43:12.673
We need to keep
them in the ground,

00:43:12.715 --> 00:43:15.050
so we've got this big
divestment campaign

00:43:15.092 --> 00:43:18.762
where we stop investing in
coal and oil and natural gas.

00:43:18.804 --> 00:43:21.599
But if we stop
investing in the stocks,

00:43:21.599 --> 00:43:24.810
as long as we continue
to fly and drive

00:43:24.852 --> 00:43:28.564
and have infrastructure built
around cheap transportation

00:43:28.606 --> 00:43:32.276
and global connectivity
of supply chains,

00:43:32.318 --> 00:43:36.280
then some hedge fund will
just buy those stocks back

00:43:36.322 --> 00:43:37.865
five cents cheaper.

00:43:37.906 --> 00:43:40.409
We can very easily get
into the mode, for example,

00:43:40.451 --> 00:43:44.038
when we think about oil,
of addiction speech.

00:43:44.079 --> 00:43:46.290
I'm going to leave this bottle
of whiskey in the cupboard,

00:43:46.290 --> 00:43:48.792
and I'm not going to touch
a drop of it, which means

00:43:48.834 --> 00:43:52.755
that I'm still fixated on
this whiskey, paradoxically.

00:43:52.796 --> 00:43:54.465
This is actually, of course,

00:43:54.506 --> 00:43:57.760
not really thinking differently.

00:43:59.178 --> 00:44:01.138
There's a little bit of
a hangover from this myth

00:44:01.180 --> 00:44:03.807
that things should be
functioning smoothly

00:44:03.807 --> 00:44:06.560
and that smooth functioning
is something that's real.

00:44:06.602 --> 00:44:09.104
[waves crash]

00:44:12.274 --> 00:44:15.778
[crickets chirp]

00:44:15.819 --> 00:44:18.155
If you find yourself
10 or 20 years from now

00:44:18.197 --> 00:44:19.990
in a world where
energy isn't cheap,

00:44:19.990 --> 00:44:22.284
you're going to have
to unwind, somehow,

00:44:22.326 --> 00:44:24.662
this massive global city system.

00:44:25.913 --> 00:44:27.998
So you look back
in history to times

00:44:27.998 --> 00:44:30.292
when this has happened before,
I mean, the Roman Empire,

00:44:30.334 --> 00:44:32.086
one of the reasons it fell

00:44:32.127 --> 00:44:33.837
was it was no longer
able to continue

00:44:33.879 --> 00:44:35.756
to bring in food and
other supplies to Rome

00:44:35.798 --> 00:44:37.549
and the other big city states.

00:44:37.591 --> 00:44:40.594
And cities no longer
were habitable,

00:44:40.636 --> 00:44:42.304
they just didn't
function anymore.

00:44:44.098 --> 00:44:45.724
So what happens
this time around?

00:44:52.398 --> 00:44:57.111
And in the past it's been
conflict with other tribes

00:44:57.152 --> 00:45:00.823
that has brought human
progress, such as it is,

00:45:00.864 --> 00:45:02.866
at a tremendous
cost of slaughter.

00:45:02.908 --> 00:45:05.661
[bombs whistle]

00:45:05.703 --> 00:45:08.539
World War I brought forth
radio and airplanes.

00:45:08.580 --> 00:45:11.208
World War II gave
us nuclear energy.

00:45:12.876 --> 00:45:15.546
Space race between the United
States and the Soviet Union

00:45:15.546 --> 00:45:18.882
and the Cold War that gave
us communication satellites

00:45:18.924 --> 00:45:22.052
and global positioning
and many other things,

00:45:22.094 --> 00:45:23.887
and now we're into something

00:45:23.887 --> 00:45:25.264
where we have to
work collectively.

00:45:25.305 --> 00:45:26.932
We can't work
against each other,

00:45:26.974 --> 00:45:28.726
we have to work with each other,

00:45:28.767 --> 00:45:33.063
we have to work with each other
with the view of the future,

00:45:33.063 --> 00:45:36.567
not against each
other in the present.

00:45:39.862 --> 00:45:42.948
The trouble is not so much
in what we're thinking

00:45:42.990 --> 00:45:44.450
about the world we live in,

00:45:44.491 --> 00:45:47.745
it's in terms of how we think
about the world we live in.

00:45:48.996 --> 00:45:52.124
[somber electronic music]
[waves crash]

00:45:52.166 --> 00:45:54.293
Well, here we have
a human brain.

00:45:54.334 --> 00:45:55.794
When you see it in reality,

00:45:55.836 --> 00:45:59.506
it seems kind of a bit
disappointing, doesn't it?

00:45:59.548 --> 00:46:01.133
It's very small
for what it does,

00:46:01.175 --> 00:46:03.010
if you think about
what we're capable of,

00:46:03.051 --> 00:46:06.597
then to think it all comes out
of a little object like that,

00:46:06.597 --> 00:46:07.723
it's quite remarkable.

00:46:09.892 --> 00:46:11.059
[Jeff] Let's take a moment

00:46:11.101 --> 00:46:13.270
to imagine entering
into a theoretical world

00:46:13.312 --> 00:46:17.191
where everything in
existence, at all scales,

00:46:17.232 --> 00:46:18.901
has equal value.

00:46:20.277 --> 00:46:26.116
From skyscrapers and trees,
to humans, cola, cups,

00:46:26.158 --> 00:46:32.456
and orangutans, shoes,
and suitcases, vessels,

00:46:32.456 --> 00:46:36.710
water, diamonds, and
dragonflies. 

00:46:37.002 --> 00:46:39.129
Even time itself.

00:46:40.047 --> 00:46:43.133
If we can imagine every
thing is intrinsically equal,

00:46:43.175 --> 00:46:47.137
then we'd know that what
we perceive as reality

00:46:48.680 --> 00:46:52.351
comes from the value judgements
that exist in our minds.

00:46:59.149 --> 00:47:00.776
If everything exists
in the same way,

00:47:00.818 --> 00:47:02.611
then we have an
interesting problem.

00:47:04.822 --> 00:47:07.616
If it's true that
even just by existing

00:47:07.658 --> 00:47:09.993
I am killing a huge
number of life forms,

00:47:09.993 --> 00:47:11.954
then I have to figure out
what kinds of life forms

00:47:11.995 --> 00:47:13.330
I'd like to kill,

00:47:13.372 --> 00:47:15.374
and I have to be rather
conscious and explicit about it,

00:47:15.415 --> 00:47:17.251
and being conscious and
explicit about stuff

00:47:17.292 --> 00:47:19.336
is an incredible drag.

00:47:20.838 --> 00:47:22.631
There's always an
unintended consequence

00:47:22.673 --> 00:47:24.341
of what you're
doing, for example.

00:47:27.177 --> 00:47:29.179
[chemicals hiss]

00:47:30.597 --> 00:47:32.099
[water rushing]

00:47:32.808 --> 00:47:35.185
Reality is happening on a
number of different scales,

00:47:35.227 --> 00:47:36.436
all at once.

00:47:36.478 --> 00:47:38.647
You can never get
it completely right,

00:47:38.689 --> 00:47:41.108
so we're confronted
again with the notion

00:47:41.149 --> 00:47:45.112
of exploring various
different shades of hypocrisy.

00:47:45.153 --> 00:47:48.156
You know the famous study,
if I offer you $20 now,

00:47:48.198 --> 00:47:50.951
or I can give you
$50 in a week's time,

00:47:50.993 --> 00:47:53.579
people just kind of are almost,

00:47:53.620 --> 00:47:55.038
kind of compelled to
go for the short term.

00:47:55.038 --> 00:47:56.206
To go for the short term.

00:47:56.248 --> 00:47:57.916
Yeah, if you think
about it, up to a point,

00:47:57.958 --> 00:47:59.334
that's very rational.
-Mm.

00:47:59.376 --> 00:48:00.794
Because you could be dead.

00:48:00.836 --> 00:48:02.546
That's right, you don't know
who's going to [laughs].

00:48:02.588 --> 00:48:03.380
Right?

00:48:03.422 --> 00:48:04.590
So, the

00:48:04.631 --> 00:48:06.174
Bird in the hand,
worth two in the bush.

00:48:06.216 --> 00:48:07.801
Absolutely, so there
is some rationality

00:48:07.843 --> 00:48:09.052
to that discounting.

00:48:09.052 --> 00:48:11.054
Organisms that worried
about 50 years from now

00:48:11.054 --> 00:48:13.056
were outcompeted by organisms

00:48:13.098 --> 00:48:15.142
that worried about
5 minutes from now.

00:48:18.145 --> 00:48:20.188
Our fate is in our own hands.

00:48:22.441 --> 00:48:23.942
No one else.

00:48:25.402 --> 00:48:28.071
[wind whistles]

00:48:30.324 --> 00:48:35.329
[thunder rumbles]
[ambient instrumental music]

00:48:35.787 --> 00:48:37.497
[Jeff] How will we
shape the future

00:48:37.539 --> 00:48:38.790
towards a better outcome?

00:48:40.918 --> 00:48:43.295
What defines our identity?

00:48:45.714 --> 00:48:48.133
[bird chatters]

00:48:48.175 --> 00:48:49.092
Are we our urges?

00:48:55.223 --> 00:48:56.975
Are we our principles?

00:49:02.898 --> 00:49:04.066
Who are we?

00:49:07.527 --> 00:49:13.784
Who I am is really the
accumulation of my experiences

00:49:13.825 --> 00:49:17.329
with other people, my
interactions with other people.

00:49:18.413 --> 00:49:20.624
Babies don't have
a "self" concept,

00:49:20.666 --> 00:49:23.460
they don't have an
idea of who they are.

00:49:24.628 --> 00:49:27.130
But as they interact
with other people,

00:49:27.130 --> 00:49:30.467
they begin to create
an image of themselves,

00:49:31.593 --> 00:49:34.304
a feeling of who they are, and
thoughts about who they are.

00:49:35.514 --> 00:49:39.101
That's really picked up
from the views and responses

00:49:39.142 --> 00:49:39.977
of other people.

00:49:41.979 --> 00:49:43.397
In this modern society,

00:49:43.438 --> 00:49:45.232
people spend a large
amount of time and effort

00:49:45.273 --> 00:49:47.609
accumulating objects
and possessions,

00:49:47.651 --> 00:49:50.654
and sometimes these have no
particular use, whatsoever.

00:49:50.654 --> 00:49:53.824
But it's almost as if we have
to acquire things around us

00:49:53.865 --> 00:49:56.368
to make a statement
of who we are.

00:49:56.410 --> 00:49:59.788
We use objects as an
extension of ourself.

00:49:59.830 --> 00:50:04.835
In the life of sentient
beings, desire is inevitable.

00:50:05.210 --> 00:50:08.005
The kind of plastic products
that consumerism makes

00:50:08.046 --> 00:50:12.509
out of desire might be optional,
but desire is inevitable.

00:50:12.509 --> 00:50:15.721
That same drive is what makes
it easy for Apple to sell us

00:50:15.762 --> 00:50:17.305
a new iPhone every year.

00:50:17.347 --> 00:50:20.684
I mean, I guarantee you, you
don't need the next iPhone.

00:50:20.684 --> 00:50:22.853
In fact, you don't even
know what new features

00:50:22.853 --> 00:50:25.647
it's going to have, and you
won't know, until you see them.

00:50:25.689 --> 00:50:27.607
But you're pretty sure
you're going to like them

00:50:27.649 --> 00:50:29.026
when you see them.

00:50:29.026 --> 00:50:30.777
And that was the whole
point of this notion

00:50:30.819 --> 00:50:32.571
of dynamic obsolescence.

00:50:33.572 --> 00:50:35.198
You would be allowed to feel,

00:50:35.198 --> 00:50:37.117
to achieve a certain
emotional state,

00:50:37.159 --> 00:50:38.618
for a short period of time,

00:50:38.660 --> 00:50:40.829
and then almost as quickly
as you'd achieved it,

00:50:40.871 --> 00:50:42.039
it would begin fading,

00:50:42.080 --> 00:50:43.498
and you'd have to
have something else.

00:50:46.168 --> 00:50:49.004
We have a real love
for a way to feel

00:50:49.046 --> 00:50:50.547
how we want to feel faster.

00:50:53.675 --> 00:50:56.970
We may have a very developed
conscious mind today,

00:50:57.012 --> 00:50:59.598
with a lot of knowledge,
cultural knowledge,

00:50:59.639 --> 00:51:01.391
technical knowledge,

00:51:01.433 --> 00:51:03.769
but the way we think is
still very much influenced

00:51:03.810 --> 00:51:06.480
by a primitive unconscious
that we developed

00:51:06.521 --> 00:51:08.148
millions of years ago.

00:51:15.739 --> 00:51:20.577
[Jeff] We humans aren't just
like animals, we are animals.

00:51:20.619 --> 00:51:23.455
And as animals, we
compete for mates.

00:51:24.664 --> 00:51:27.542
[percussive music]

00:51:29.211 --> 00:51:33.090
Organisms in the wild that have
extra resources to display,

00:51:33.131 --> 00:51:35.926
like flashy tails,
or large antlers,

00:51:35.926 --> 00:51:38.512
are advertising to their mates

00:51:38.553 --> 00:51:43.391
that my genes are so good, I
don't need to skimp and save,

00:51:43.433 --> 00:51:47.229
because I'm so strong, I have
these amazing attributes.

00:51:48.605 --> 00:51:51.441
The same phenomenon
happens in human societies

00:51:51.483 --> 00:51:54.111
to impress members
of the opposite sex.

00:51:56.446 --> 00:52:00.450
A lot of these displays
require spending extra energy

00:52:00.450 --> 00:52:03.036
and natural resources
that we don't really need

00:52:03.078 --> 00:52:04.704
to be genetically fit,

00:52:05.956 --> 00:52:08.166
but we respond to
those cultural signals

00:52:08.208 --> 00:52:11.002
as if those things
really do matter.

00:52:14.214 --> 00:52:17.801
This question of status
and of showing our status,

00:52:17.801 --> 00:52:19.094
I do believe

00:52:19.136 --> 00:52:22.180
that that's one of the most
fundamental determinants

00:52:22.222 --> 00:52:23.014
of our behavior.

00:52:23.140 --> 00:52:24.891
-Yeah. Well you know
who knows this

00:52:24.933 --> 00:52:27.394
best of all, is the marketing
and the advertising people.

00:52:27.435 --> 00:52:28.854
Because when they're
selling us stuff,

00:52:28.895 --> 00:52:31.982
and it's designer, or,
what they're tapping into

00:52:31.982 --> 00:52:35.360
is this preoccupation with
what other people think,

00:52:35.402 --> 00:52:36.945
because they
understand, intuitively,

00:52:36.987 --> 00:52:40.157
that we're all animals
seeking social status.

00:52:46.163 --> 00:52:49.875
We like things to be convenient,
we like things to be new,

00:52:49.916 --> 00:52:52.794
and we like bags that
have names on it.

00:52:52.836 --> 00:52:55.463
We like to drink out of
bottles that we can throw away.

00:52:55.505 --> 00:52:58.800
We like styrofoam containers
for our fast food.

00:53:00.218 --> 00:53:05.056
Plastic itself is something
that is persistent through time.

00:53:05.098 --> 00:53:07.475
It gets recycled, it gets
recycled, it gets smaller,

00:53:07.517 --> 00:53:10.145
it gets smaller, and now
it's right back to us

00:53:10.187 --> 00:53:13.523
and detectable, in
our blood stream.

00:53:13.565 --> 00:53:16.568
[whale whistles and groans]

00:53:22.574 --> 00:53:24.201
[seal barking]

00:53:25.076 --> 00:53:28.496
We're out of alignment
with what we hear

00:53:28.538 --> 00:53:31.583
and we understand, and what we
know is going on,

00:53:31.625 --> 00:53:34.920
and the life that I am leading,

00:53:34.961 --> 00:53:37.047
including all the things I love

00:53:37.088 --> 00:53:40.842
that might be really
ecologically problematic

00:53:40.884 --> 00:53:42.928
but that I love them anyways.

00:53:42.969 --> 00:53:46.389
So we have this very
positive spin on ourself,

00:53:46.431 --> 00:53:49.184
and we try to maintain
that characterization

00:53:49.226 --> 00:53:52.562
to the extent that we will only
pay attention to information

00:53:52.604 --> 00:53:54.147
which confirms that bias.

00:53:55.315 --> 00:53:57.609
[gentle electronic music]

00:53:57.651 --> 00:54:00.737
We'll deliberately reframe
things that we've done

00:54:00.779 --> 00:54:04.157
in order to keep the
coherence of who we are.

00:54:08.286 --> 00:54:11.289
[audience applauds]

00:54:15.919 --> 00:54:19.089
Our brains are always
creating these distortions.

00:54:19.089 --> 00:54:21.758
We have no direct
contact with reality.

00:54:21.800 --> 00:54:24.094
One of the important
qualities that we developed

00:54:24.094 --> 00:54:26.221
that helped us survive
was group identity.

00:54:27.764 --> 00:54:30.433
And today, group identity
still plays an important role

00:54:30.433 --> 00:54:33.603
in our life and has an
important unconscious effect

00:54:33.603 --> 00:54:35.939
on our attitudes
and our actions.

00:54:35.939 --> 00:54:37.732
So we have this tendency always

00:54:37.774 --> 00:54:39.567
to attribute blame to others,

00:54:39.609 --> 00:54:41.361
rather than accepting
it ourselves.

00:54:41.403 --> 00:54:42.946
And this is part of
the bias which creates

00:54:42.988 --> 00:54:46.449
or keeps the coherence of
this being a good person.

00:54:49.369 --> 00:54:51.246
[Jeff] So, we're all
capable of running around

00:54:51.288 --> 00:54:55.583
with the wrong idea of what is
really going on in our world.

00:54:55.625 --> 00:54:58.503
And, there's no mechanism
or voice inside us

00:54:58.545 --> 00:55:00.672
to signal the
artificiality of that.

00:55:02.257 --> 00:55:04.467
We don't want to live
in a state of anxiety,

00:55:05.510 --> 00:55:08.138
we want to believe that
the things we enjoy doing

00:55:08.179 --> 00:55:09.306
will never change.

00:55:15.020 --> 00:55:18.273
Do we confuse what we
need with what we desire,

00:55:18.315 --> 00:55:20.984
and what we desire,
with what we need?

00:55:23.987 --> 00:55:27.240
Desire is logically
prior to need, actually,

00:55:27.282 --> 00:55:30.994
and need is really, just,
like stabilized desire

00:55:30.994 --> 00:55:32.662
that's been turned,
probably socially,

00:55:32.704 --> 00:55:35.707
into something
seemingly necessary.

00:55:35.749 --> 00:55:37.459
And so I think we need to
be a little bit careful

00:55:37.500 --> 00:55:40.587
when we talk about being
in an ecological society,

00:55:40.628 --> 00:55:43.631
meaning our needs matching
up with what we want.

00:55:45.633 --> 00:55:47.093
So there's this constant dance

00:55:47.135 --> 00:55:51.139
between the inhibitory centers
that know what's good for us

00:55:51.181 --> 00:55:52.223
in the top of the brain, 

00:55:52.265 --> 00:55:54.059
particularly the
prefrontal area,

00:55:54.100 --> 00:55:57.354
and the midbrain areas,
for emotional impulse,

00:55:57.395 --> 00:56:00.690
which just wants to grab that
yummy thing, and have it now.

00:56:01.900 --> 00:56:05.028
So, let's say we're in a bakery,
everything looks so good,

00:56:05.070 --> 00:56:08.156
and our brain, which
during evolution

00:56:08.198 --> 00:56:12.243
was designed to crave fat
and sugar, is going nuts.

00:56:12.285 --> 00:56:15.372
It just really wants one
of those yummy things.

00:56:15.372 --> 00:56:17.499
But then, our
prefrontal cortex,

00:56:17.540 --> 00:56:19.209
the part of the
brain that learns,

00:56:19.250 --> 00:56:20.794
the part of the brain
that understands,

00:56:20.835 --> 00:56:24.339
says to us, you know,
this is fattening,

00:56:24.381 --> 00:56:26.591
and you have that
blood sugar problem,

00:56:26.633 --> 00:56:28.718
it's not so great for you.

00:56:28.760 --> 00:56:31.388
And the prefrontal
cortex has the ability

00:56:31.388 --> 00:56:34.265
to inhibit emotional impulse,

00:56:34.307 --> 00:56:37.560
and once we understand the
true cost of the things we use

00:56:37.560 --> 00:56:40.021
and the things we do
to the environment,

00:56:40.063 --> 00:56:42.065
the very same mechanism applies,

00:56:43.483 --> 00:56:46.903
and it let's us pull back
to the balcony of the mind,

00:56:46.903 --> 00:56:48.905
where we see everything 
that's going on,

00:56:48.905 --> 00:56:51.199
then we can make
a better decision.

00:56:51.241 --> 00:56:55.036
This is the first step
in changing our habits

00:56:55.078 --> 00:56:56.371
because without mindfulness,

00:56:56.413 --> 00:56:58.832
we don't even notice
the choice points,

00:56:58.873 --> 00:57:03.169
we just blindly, spontaneously,
automatically do that thing

00:57:03.211 --> 00:57:06.923
we've always done, our
old habits take us over.

00:57:06.965 --> 00:57:08.508
When there's uncertainty,

00:57:08.550 --> 00:57:11.719
when there's a
sense of precarity,

00:57:11.761 --> 00:57:16.766
when there's a feeling of
vulnerability and confusion,

00:57:17.809 --> 00:57:23.398
that's exactly when we are
most susceptible, obviously,

00:57:23.440 --> 00:57:27.444
to adhering to a message,
a voice, a person

00:57:28.153 --> 00:57:33.741
who comes along and is able
to meet those anxieties

00:57:33.783 --> 00:57:38.663
and those uncertainties with
such confidence and certainty.

00:57:39.831 --> 00:57:42.625
And so, that's what we've seen
replaying over and over again

00:57:42.625 --> 00:57:43.835
in politics.

00:57:44.794 --> 00:57:47.630
[audience cheers]

00:57:51.176 --> 00:57:53.303
I don't need to mention
the obvious examples

00:57:53.344 --> 00:58:00.852
of what happens when the
larger public's sense of fear

00:58:01.978 --> 00:58:03.354
is coopted.

00:58:03.730 --> 00:58:05.231
About half the wealth
in the country is held

00:58:05.273 --> 00:58:06.608
by the top 1% of the people,

00:58:06.649 --> 00:58:09.736
and there are a lot
of us out there,

00:58:09.777 --> 00:58:11.488
and my parents were
certainly one of them,

00:58:11.529 --> 00:58:14.073
who were scrambling from
day to day to survive.

00:58:14.115 --> 00:58:16.659
And they, in their
lifetimes, in the 1930s,

00:58:16.701 --> 00:58:18.203
had actually seen
people starving

00:58:18.244 --> 00:58:20.330
because they couldn't
earn a living.

00:58:20.330 --> 00:58:23.875
And so, most Americans
haven't seen that,

00:58:23.917 --> 00:58:26.169
but some Americans
still feel that way.

00:58:26.211 --> 00:58:29.964
And they feel the threat and
the fear of unemployment,

00:58:30.006 --> 00:58:32.717
so they're focused nearterm.

00:58:35.094 --> 00:58:36.513
In any symbiotic relationship,

00:58:36.554 --> 00:58:38.765
who's the top and who's the
bottom is a little bit

00:58:38.806 --> 00:58:41.518
confusing, and this could
collapse at any moment.

00:58:47.440 --> 00:58:51.861
The desire to be rescued
is based on a profound

00:58:51.861 --> 00:58:55.490
and deep sense of powerlessness.

00:58:55.532 --> 00:58:58.284
[crickets chirp]

00:58:59.285 --> 00:59:00.537
Open up a paper,

00:59:00.537 --> 00:59:02.830
or watch a conventional
ecological documentary,

00:59:02.872 --> 00:59:06.918
and what you'll see
are all kinds of facts

00:59:06.960 --> 00:59:10.171
designed to make you feel
scared, and upset, and guilty.

00:59:11.256 --> 00:59:14.801
This is how we talk to ourselves
about ecological disaster.

00:59:14.842 --> 00:59:17.637
Now the trouble is, that
means that psychologically,

00:59:17.679 --> 00:59:19.013
we're actually still
putting ourselves

00:59:19.055 --> 00:59:21.057
before a time when it happened,

00:59:21.099 --> 00:59:23.226
somehow allowing
ourselves the choice

00:59:23.268 --> 00:59:24.894
to either go down
that path or not.

00:59:24.894 --> 00:59:27.230
Now, the point is, we're
already down the path.

00:59:30.441 --> 00:59:32.610
When we, on the
climate science side,

00:59:32.652 --> 00:59:34.821
talk about the reality
of climate change,

00:59:34.862 --> 00:59:38.116
at first blush it sounds
like a bit of a downer.

00:59:38.157 --> 00:59:42.787
So, one's natural reaction
if you hear bad news

00:59:42.829 --> 00:59:44.998
and someone else tells you,
no, no no, that's not true,

00:59:45.039 --> 00:59:47.667
is to at least,
sort of say, hope,

00:59:47.709 --> 00:59:49.085
that the guy who is telling you

00:59:49.085 --> 00:59:51.838
that this is not going
to happen is right.

00:59:51.879 --> 00:59:54.007
For six years in Congress,

00:59:54.048 --> 00:59:55.883
I said that climate
change was nonsense.

00:59:55.925 --> 00:59:57.844
I really didn't know
anything about it.

01:00:00.263 --> 01:00:02.432
Since I represented probably
the reddest district

01:00:02.432 --> 01:00:04.392
in the reddest
state in the nation,

01:00:04.434 --> 01:00:07.145
I knew that I needed
to be opposed to it.

01:00:08.730 --> 01:00:10.481
And my son came to me.

01:00:10.523 --> 01:00:12.567
He was voting for the first
time, he had just turned 18,

01:00:12.609 --> 01:00:16.112
and he said to me, "Dad,
I'll vote for you,

01:00:16.112 --> 01:00:18.489
"but you're going to clean up
your act on the environment."

01:00:19.032 --> 01:00:21.284
Your child is sick.

01:00:21.326 --> 01:00:24.662
98 doctors say,
treat him this way.

01:00:24.704 --> 01:00:28.958
Two say, no, this
other is the way to go.

01:00:29.000 --> 01:00:30.043
I'll go with the two.

01:00:31.294 --> 01:00:33.046
You're takin' a big
risk for those kids.

01:00:34.380 --> 01:00:35.798
Some people think
that what we have here

01:00:35.840 --> 01:00:38.051
is an information deficit.

01:00:38.092 --> 01:00:41.262
That if we just gave people
more scientific information,

01:00:41.304 --> 01:00:44.390
showed them the science,
then they would come around.

01:00:44.432 --> 01:00:47.018
We've got people
that make a living

01:00:47.060 --> 01:00:50.521
and a lot of money on
talk radio and talk TV,

01:00:51.439 --> 01:00:52.815
pronouncing all kinds of things.

01:00:52.815 --> 01:00:55.610
They slept at Holiday
Inn Express last night,

01:00:55.652 --> 01:00:57.862
and they are now
experts on climate.

01:00:57.904 --> 01:01:00.323
It's an identification problem.

01:01:00.323 --> 01:01:02.825
It's, it doesn't
look like my tribe,

01:01:02.825 --> 01:01:04.327
doesn't sound like my tribe,

01:01:04.327 --> 01:01:06.079
doesn't sound like our war cry,

01:01:06.120 --> 01:01:07.664
doesn't sound like our song.

01:01:10.500 --> 01:01:13.503
Too often it's presented,
climate science is presented,

01:01:13.503 --> 01:01:15.588
as if it's some kind
of new religion,

01:01:15.630 --> 01:01:17.131
and you have to believe.

01:01:18.508 --> 01:01:22.053
No, don't ask people to
believe in climate science.

01:01:22.095 --> 01:01:23.805
Just say, here's some data.

01:01:23.846 --> 01:01:27.225
Now, what does your
faith tradition tell you

01:01:27.266 --> 01:01:29.644
about what to do with that data?

01:01:29.686 --> 01:01:31.104
In mine, it tells me

01:01:31.145 --> 01:01:34.357
that I'm a steward of
this glorious creation.

01:01:34.399 --> 01:01:36.526
Sometimes science
turns out to be wrong.

01:01:37.694 --> 01:01:39.779
But other times it turns
out to be very right.

01:01:40.571 --> 01:01:42.281
And the key to
scientific endeavors,

01:01:42.323 --> 01:01:45.451
what we're here to
discuss today, is openness,

01:01:45.493 --> 01:01:52.083
access to the data, and full
challenging of the data.

01:01:52.208 --> 01:01:54.544
That's how we advance science.

01:01:54.585 --> 01:01:56.796
I look forward to hearing
Mr. Chairman, thank you.

01:01:56.838 --> 01:01:57.880
[Chairman] Thank you.

01:01:57.922 --> 01:02:00.883
There have been major
revolutions in our thinking

01:02:00.883 --> 01:02:04.053
across civilization, for
example, for thousands of years,

01:02:04.095 --> 01:02:05.722
it was thought that the
Earth was at the center

01:02:05.763 --> 01:02:07.223
of the universe.

01:02:07.265 --> 01:02:08.516
We now know that
that's not true.

01:02:08.558 --> 01:02:10.852
And then of course,
there was also the idea

01:02:10.893 --> 01:02:14.272
that man is the pinnacle
of the animal kingdom,

01:02:14.313 --> 01:02:17.066
but now we discover, through
natural selection and Darwinism,

01:02:17.066 --> 01:02:19.527
that we are just one
of a number of species

01:02:19.569 --> 01:02:21.237
that have existed,

01:02:21.237 --> 01:02:23.156
and finally if you
think about the self

01:02:23.197 --> 01:02:25.825
and the idea that we are
individuals in control

01:02:25.867 --> 01:02:27.827
of all our actions
and behaviors,

01:02:27.869 --> 01:02:30.830
that has been eroded away
as we come to discover

01:02:30.872 --> 01:02:34.709
that this, in fact, is
also not entirely true.

01:02:34.751 --> 01:02:36.919
So, I think that the
science has revealed

01:02:36.961 --> 01:02:38.838
the arrogance of
the individualism,

01:02:38.880 --> 01:02:41.883
the egocentrism that we
have about our planet,

01:02:41.924 --> 01:02:45.386
our species, and
ourselves, is unwarranted.

01:02:50.641 --> 01:02:51.476
[Jeff] Ecology.

01:02:52.602 --> 01:02:54.103
It's a word derived
from the Greek

01:02:54.145 --> 01:02:56.773
oikos, or household.

01:02:57.940 --> 01:02:59.650
It's the study of
the relationships

01:02:59.692 --> 01:03:03.738
that interlink all the members
of the Earth's household.

01:03:09.452 --> 01:03:12.538
We can't think of ecology
as only existing over there,

01:03:12.580 --> 01:03:15.541
beyond, because it
has no boundaries,

01:03:15.583 --> 01:03:18.085
and is in a state
of constant flux.

01:03:19.504 --> 01:03:23.633
Ecology is intimate, coming
right up to our skin,

01:03:23.633 --> 01:03:25.176
and through it.

01:03:25.218 --> 01:03:29.847
It's permeable and
borderless and chaotic.

01:03:32.183 --> 01:03:36.145
The internal and the
external are always entwined.

01:03:37.313 --> 01:03:40.316
[traffic hums]
[horns honk]

01:03:40.316 --> 01:03:42.735
So when we speak
about sustainability,

01:03:43.903 --> 01:03:47.156
what is it that we
hope to sustain?

01:04:00.628 --> 01:04:02.046
One of the problems
with philosophy

01:04:02.088 --> 01:04:03.840
is that it isn't
just in your head.

01:04:05.383 --> 01:04:07.844
Philosophy is everywhere.

01:04:07.885 --> 01:04:11.931
Everything, in a way, was
a dream in someone's head,

01:04:11.973 --> 01:04:13.516
in built space.

01:04:15.393 --> 01:04:16.936
Thought isn't just
something that's in here,

01:04:16.978 --> 01:04:18.062
thought is everywhere.

01:04:20.982 --> 01:04:23.526
The story that we live
in right now is that

01:04:23.568 --> 01:04:28.906
we grow the economy
by consuming goods

01:04:29.240 --> 01:04:33.953
and in particular,
consuming goods that exist

01:04:33.995 --> 01:04:35.621
in limited quantities.

01:04:35.663 --> 01:04:39.375
We have the tendency
as meaning-making,

01:04:39.417 --> 01:04:45.715
story-making people,
creatures, to have characters,

01:04:45.715 --> 01:04:48.759
to have protagonists
who are the villains,

01:04:48.801 --> 01:04:51.888
and who are the heroes
and the rescuers,

01:04:51.929 --> 01:04:55.808
and that's deep in
our human psyche,

01:04:55.850 --> 01:04:59.145
and that's how we tend to
make sense of the world.

01:04:59.186 --> 01:05:02.565
And the tendency,
very understandably,

01:05:02.607 --> 01:05:07.612
is to create villains,
such as corporations

01:05:08.988 --> 01:05:12.909
or oil industries, and to
create heroes, the ecowarriors.

01:05:13.784 --> 01:05:16.746
While that's understandable,
what it's actually doing

01:05:16.787 --> 01:05:21.167
is it's creating a bit
of an exemption story

01:05:21.208 --> 01:05:25.338
where we as individuals
are somewhat exempt

01:05:25.379 --> 01:05:27.715
or somewhat out of the story.

01:05:31.928 --> 01:05:34.639
There's a lot of criticism
in our culture right now

01:05:34.680 --> 01:05:40.519
that capitalism is bad, and
that without capitalism,

01:05:40.561 --> 01:05:42.188
we wouldn't be impacting things.

01:05:42.229 --> 01:05:43.522
And that's partially true.

01:05:49.570 --> 01:05:51.447
[Jeff] Underpinning this system

01:05:51.489 --> 01:05:54.283
is the optimal foraging
instinct that resides

01:05:54.325 --> 01:05:55.910
in our animal natures.

01:05:58.329 --> 01:06:02.625
Wolves, for instance. They're
better off running down an elk

01:06:02.625 --> 01:06:05.211
than spending the same
amount of time and energy

01:06:05.252 --> 01:06:07.296
chasing a mouse, or a rabbit.

01:06:09.590 --> 01:06:11.592
They're more evolutionarily fit

01:06:11.634 --> 01:06:14.303
and have more calories
to raise their offspring.

01:06:14.303 --> 01:06:17.306
[wolf howls]

01:06:17.348 --> 01:06:19.308
The same dynamic is in humans.

01:06:20.518 --> 01:06:23.562
We like to invest a little
and get more in return.

01:06:25.064 --> 01:06:27.274
We do this in stock markets,

01:06:27.316 --> 01:06:29.819
when we get a 20%
discount on shoes,

01:06:31.529 --> 01:06:34.532
a two-for-one cocktail
at happy hour,

01:06:34.573 --> 01:06:36.659
or a steal of a
deal buying a house

01:06:36.659 --> 01:06:38.577
on some special foreclosure.

01:06:39.745 --> 01:06:44.458
Capitalism isn't
entirely good or bad.

01:06:44.500 --> 01:06:48.087
Capitalism is in service
of the superorganism.

01:06:50.631 --> 01:06:54.010
Together, we're functioning
like a gigantic wolf pack,

01:06:54.051 --> 01:06:56.053
hungry for more energy.

01:07:01.517 --> 01:07:03.602
You know, one of the
many causes of war

01:07:03.644 --> 01:07:05.813
is actually the
shortage of energy.

01:07:06.522 --> 01:07:09.358
[explosion booms]

01:07:14.697 --> 01:07:16.198
Since energy is always flowing

01:07:16.240 --> 01:07:20.703
from a kind of compacted stated
to a less compacted state,

01:07:20.703 --> 01:07:23.456
[explosion booms]

01:07:23.497 --> 01:07:26.792
entropy is how things are
constantly running down to zero.

01:07:27.835 --> 01:07:30.588
We can't actually create a
machine that reverses entropy.

01:07:32.006 --> 01:07:36.177
Entropy is why time seems to
only be going in one direction.

01:07:38.554 --> 01:07:42.391
Entropy is why you've
never seen a broken glass

01:07:42.433 --> 01:07:44.060
reassemble as a perfect glass.

01:07:45.186 --> 01:07:48.022
And if you're looking for the
perfect ecological machine

01:07:48.064 --> 01:07:51.275
that will actually fully
put the energy back

01:07:51.317 --> 01:07:54.361
in to the system that the
machine has sucked out of it,

01:07:54.403 --> 01:07:55.571
I'm afraid you're
going to be waiting

01:07:55.571 --> 01:07:57.073
until after the end
of the universe.

01:08:06.665 --> 01:08:08.584
So, you always
have to leave room

01:08:08.626 --> 01:08:12.088
in your analysis of animals,
and animals in the broad sense,

01:08:12.129 --> 01:08:13.881
I include people in that,

01:08:13.923 --> 01:08:16.801
of thinking that we're always
going to do the right thing,

01:08:17.218 --> 01:08:20.137
that we've optimized
all of our actions,

01:08:20.179 --> 01:08:22.473
our foraging strategies
and everything else,

01:08:22.515 --> 01:08:24.225
and we can
extrapolate from there

01:08:24.266 --> 01:08:26.435
because if we had
everything right,

01:08:26.435 --> 01:08:27.895
there wouldn't be any
problems, would there?

01:08:27.937 --> 01:08:30.397
There'd be no poverty,
there'd be no climate change,

01:08:30.439 --> 01:08:32.274
there'd be no warfare.

01:08:32.316 --> 01:08:36.695
[gentle instrumental music]

01:08:36.737 --> 01:08:38.364
Where you sit determines
where you stand,

01:08:38.405 --> 01:08:41.951
and if you're sort
of all tied up

01:08:41.992 --> 01:08:43.994
in any particular
kind of industry

01:08:44.036 --> 01:08:48.207
and you see your future
there, naturally you hold on.

01:08:49.959 --> 01:08:52.378
There's no such thing
as a free market.

01:08:52.419 --> 01:08:54.171
I hope people can
understand that

01:08:54.213 --> 01:08:57.133
because if there
were free markets,

01:08:57.174 --> 01:09:02.179
you'd end up the way we
ended up in the late 1800s,

01:09:02.471 --> 01:09:06.851
with a few very powerful trusts
controlling all of America.

01:09:08.936 --> 01:09:13.482
Without government helping
us shape the market forces,

01:09:13.524 --> 01:09:16.694
the market forces will be taken

01:09:16.735 --> 01:09:19.488
by the very shortterm
requirements

01:09:19.530 --> 01:09:22.616
that are placed on them by us.

01:09:22.658 --> 01:09:24.618
By our need to go to
the filling station

01:09:24.660 --> 01:09:28.164
and get the cheapest fuel, by
the need for the pension fund

01:09:28.164 --> 01:09:31.959
to get its 8% return, no
matter what it's investing in.

01:09:33.169 --> 01:09:37.464
Really tough to earn the
6 or 7% or 8% per year

01:09:37.506 --> 01:09:40.134
that were in those
actuarial calculations

01:09:40.176 --> 01:09:41.635
when the pension funds
were established.

01:09:41.677 --> 01:09:44.930
So, it's not some
foreign institution

01:09:44.972 --> 01:09:48.767
that makes us be short term
focused, it's us.

01:09:48.809 --> 01:09:52.980
Why didn't the administration
anticipate the energy crisis

01:09:53.022 --> 01:09:56.442
several years ago, formulate
a positive action plan

01:09:56.483 --> 01:09:57.735
and do something about it?

01:09:57.902 --> 01:10:01.155
Have to bring a different
perspective to this.

01:10:02.531 --> 01:10:04.783
A couple of years ago I
asked a member of Congress,

01:10:04.825 --> 01:10:07.953
I said, "what's the toughest
issue you're dealing with,

01:10:07.995 --> 01:10:09.163
"right now?"

01:10:09.205 --> 01:10:12.124
And she said federal subsidies

01:10:12.166 --> 01:10:16.378
for seashore flood insurance

01:10:16.378 --> 01:10:19.006
because we've always
subsidized it,

01:10:19.048 --> 01:10:22.635
and the cost of the
subsidies is going way up,

01:10:22.676 --> 01:10:24.553
and people insist on
buying these homes

01:10:24.595 --> 01:10:28.682
that are subject to tidal
erosion and to flooding,

01:10:28.724 --> 01:10:30.893
and to destruction, ultimately.

01:10:32.228 --> 01:10:34.730
There isn't transparent
accountable pricing

01:10:34.730 --> 01:10:39.735
for all the impacts of the
burning of fossil fuels.

01:10:41.070 --> 01:10:43.113
The markets still
aren't working right,

01:10:43.155 --> 01:10:48.118
and once those costs are
revealed, and put at the meter,

01:10:48.160 --> 01:10:52.248
and put at the pump, then
consumers will make choices

01:10:52.289 --> 01:10:54.792
that are in their self-interest.

01:10:54.833 --> 01:10:59.255
We'll be making transportation
fuel on our roofs.

01:11:00.631 --> 01:11:02.925
Of course I'm not saying
that we get to nirvana

01:11:02.967 --> 01:11:04.593
and we don't have any war.

01:11:04.635 --> 01:11:07.429
One of the causes of
war would be less,

01:11:07.471 --> 01:11:10.391
because energy would be
more abundantly available

01:11:10.432 --> 01:11:12.309
and more distributed
around the world.

01:11:14.812 --> 01:11:17.481
[Jeff] If we're going
to live up to our name,

01:11:17.523 --> 01:11:20.776
Homo sapiens, which
means wise man,

01:11:20.818 --> 01:11:23.779
we'll have to understand our
subconscious motivations,

01:11:23.821 --> 01:11:27.658
our unconscious desires
and justifications

01:11:29.326 --> 01:11:31.495
and the true cost of energy.

01:11:36.166 --> 01:11:38.419
When you look at the past,
you're looking at a decision,

01:11:38.460 --> 01:11:41.755
decisions about how
to live in the world

01:11:41.797 --> 01:11:43.507
that people in the past made.

01:11:45.968 --> 01:11:47.303
It may be technically true

01:11:47.344 --> 01:11:49.179
that we don't know everything

01:11:49.221 --> 01:11:50.806
about where our food came from.

01:11:52.099 --> 01:11:53.475
We keep telling
ourselves it's difficult,

01:11:53.475 --> 01:11:54.977
but just read the back
of the cereal packet.

01:11:55.019 --> 01:11:56.395
It tells you what
all the stuff is,

01:11:56.437 --> 01:11:59.356
and then you can figure out
where it came from on Google.

01:12:04.653 --> 01:12:07.740
From what countries
do the raw materials

01:12:07.781 --> 01:12:10.159
for an iPhone come from?

01:12:10.159 --> 01:12:12.244
[phone beeps]
Hey there you go.

01:12:13.412 --> 01:12:16.457
Oh here, how about this,
the commodity chain.

01:12:16.498 --> 01:12:19.335
[explosion booms]

01:12:27.343 --> 01:12:30.012
We know that certain
things that we do now

01:12:30.054 --> 01:12:32.431
turn on certain
parts of our brain

01:12:32.473 --> 01:12:34.475
or inspire certain
kind of attitude

01:12:34.516 --> 01:12:37.686
or inculcates certain
kinds of response,

01:12:37.686 --> 01:12:39.438
so however we want
to talk about it.

01:12:39.480 --> 01:12:41.148
We could think of
the kind of fire

01:12:41.190 --> 01:12:44.526
that consumerism lights up
in us with all of the desires

01:12:44.568 --> 01:12:47.613
as a kind of gateway
to more desires

01:12:47.654 --> 01:12:49.573
that might be actually
more interesting

01:12:49.615 --> 01:12:52.242
and more in sync with
other life forms.

01:12:54.203 --> 01:12:57.748
When you think about the
universe as a flow of energy,

01:12:59.291 --> 01:13:01.043
then that simply changes
your whole perspective

01:13:01.043 --> 01:13:03.045
about what it is to be alive.

01:13:06.256 --> 01:13:10.010
All living systems require
energy as individuals.

01:13:10.052 --> 01:13:11.970
If we think more strategically

01:13:12.012 --> 01:13:13.806
about how we're using
the planet's energy,

01:13:13.847 --> 01:13:16.266
life would be better
for every living thing.

01:13:17.684 --> 01:13:20.020
You know, individual
behavior can emerge

01:13:20.062 --> 01:13:22.981
as something different
at a different scale.

01:13:23.023 --> 01:13:25.275
Emergence is sort
of interactive.

01:13:26.610 --> 01:13:30.114
We don't need 24/7
access to energy.

01:13:30.948 --> 01:13:34.660
We need 24/7 access to feelings.

01:13:34.701 --> 01:13:38.914
If we can just approach
each other without the sort of

01:13:38.914 --> 01:13:42.418
condescension that
comes way too often

01:13:42.418 --> 01:13:44.294
when we come to
environmental topics,

01:13:44.336 --> 01:13:47.464
and say, all right, so we're
looking for something to work.

01:13:48.132 --> 01:13:49.341
One of the important aspects

01:13:49.383 --> 01:13:52.094
of the evolution of
cooperation is the development

01:13:52.094 --> 01:13:54.430
of complex moral emotions,

01:13:54.471 --> 01:13:57.307
so sympathy, empathy,
compassion,

01:13:57.349 --> 01:13:58.350
and these are the key.

01:13:58.434 --> 01:14:00.602
I think this is vital
to our understanding

01:14:00.602 --> 01:14:02.646
of the human condition.

01:14:04.356 --> 01:14:06.150
The morphological
adaptations that we have

01:14:06.191 --> 01:14:08.444
are simply reflections of where
our ancestors

01:14:08.485 --> 01:14:10.112
arose on the planet.

01:14:10.112 --> 01:14:13.073
How much sun did they get, how
much oxygen was in the air?

01:14:13.115 --> 01:14:14.825
These determine the
shapes of their skulls,

01:14:14.867 --> 01:14:16.326
the color of their skin,

01:14:16.368 --> 01:14:18.203
but it has nothing to do
with the inherent essence

01:14:18.245 --> 01:14:20.372
of what it means to be human

01:14:20.414 --> 01:14:22.291
because we're all
the same species.

01:14:24.585 --> 01:14:27.671
[Command] Ignition
sequence three, two.

01:14:27.713 --> 01:14:29.923
It's been a pretty wonderful
journey for mankind,

01:14:29.965 --> 01:14:32.926
but, boy, what a
wonderful set of issues

01:14:32.968 --> 01:14:35.804
to have to wrestle
with, going forward.

01:14:35.846 --> 01:14:38.056
We don't even have to
get to Alpha Centauri

01:14:38.098 --> 01:14:41.268
to find these issues and
learn about ourselves.

01:14:41.310 --> 01:14:44.480
It's about how do we look
at longterm problems.

01:14:44.521 --> 01:14:50.360
Can we embody the wisdom
that we feel's inherent here

01:14:50.402 --> 01:14:52.946
and move into the future wisely?

01:14:54.823 --> 01:14:56.450
[crickets chirp]

01:14:56.492 --> 01:15:01.413
In our council at Onondaga,
when we looked into the future,

01:15:01.455 --> 01:15:03.916
we address ourselves
and say Swyonisu.

01:15:05.542 --> 01:15:07.336
Across the fire. Swyonisu

01:15:11.507 --> 01:15:15.135
And I asked one of the
old chiefs, my uncle,

01:15:15.177 --> 01:15:17.346
I said, what does
that actually mean?

01:15:17.387 --> 01:15:21.350
And he said it means
the leaders, I said no,

01:15:21.391 --> 01:15:24.102
what's the real,
literal translation?

01:15:24.144 --> 01:15:27.731
He says it means you who
look far in the future.

01:15:31.610 --> 01:15:34.112
[Command] Now, the lift off.

01:15:34.154 --> 01:15:38.367
With this, it's gonna be made
here, the eagle has landed.

01:15:38.408 --> 01:15:41.036
Astronaut Neil Armstrong,

01:15:41.036 --> 01:15:44.289
pursuing the next step in the
evolution of our knowledge,

01:15:44.331 --> 01:15:46.875
when he returned
from one of mankind's

01:15:46.875 --> 01:15:50.212
greatest engineering
achievements, stated,

01:15:50.254 --> 01:15:52.548
"I felt the successful
lunar landing

01:15:52.548 --> 01:15:54.883
"might inspire men
around the world

01:15:54.925 --> 01:15:59.054
"to believe that impossible
goals are possible,

01:15:59.096 --> 01:16:02.558
"that there really is hope
for the future of mankind."

01:16:04.726 --> 01:16:08.397
We are living in
the future's past.

01:16:08.438 --> 01:16:12.526
The relative odds of future
outcomes change every second.

01:16:13.735 --> 01:16:15.070
The world we all live in

01:16:15.112 --> 01:16:18.490
is not larger than
the sum of its parts.

01:16:18.532 --> 01:16:21.034
[birds chirp]

01:16:21.076 --> 01:16:24.913
Each part is neither
the center or the edge.

01:16:24.955 --> 01:16:29.459
It's a bustling world,
a household of beings.

01:16:29.501 --> 01:16:33.255
Colleagues, both
human and nonhuman,

01:16:33.297 --> 01:16:35.257
animate and inanimate,

01:16:35.299 --> 01:16:39.595
over which we have influence,
but, in turn, influence us.

01:16:41.972 --> 01:16:45.559
Physical realities exist,
regardless of our desires.

01:16:48.687 --> 01:16:52.357
Seemingly insignificant
actions taken collectively

01:16:52.399 --> 01:16:54.276
have led us to this moment.

01:16:55.444 --> 01:16:59.114
[gentle instrumental music]

01:17:00.240 --> 01:17:04.453
Everything is a system
of relationships,

01:17:04.494 --> 01:17:06.330
and it's not easy to
see the connections.

01:17:07.956 --> 01:17:10.459
The connections between
the paint on a barn,

01:17:12.294 --> 01:17:16.798
the indifferent asphalt,
a press conference,

01:17:16.840 --> 01:17:20.677
or to see the invisible links
between human expectations,

01:17:23.138 --> 01:17:24.181
human vulnerability,

01:17:27.142 --> 01:17:28.143
human memory,

01:17:31.063 --> 01:17:33.482
and the fragility
of life itself.

01:17:36.777 --> 01:17:38.737
No, it requires effort.

01:17:41.698 --> 01:17:44.785
Energy comes in many forms.

01:17:44.826 --> 01:17:47.746
It's both the means and the
ends of all our pursuits.

01:17:51.124 --> 01:17:54.294
All of us held in the
arms of the atmosphere.

01:17:57.172 --> 01:17:59.841
We don't have to be
poets, scientists,

01:17:59.841 --> 01:18:02.969
superheroes, or saints.

01:18:03.011 --> 01:18:06.014
Each of us can think
about how we think.

01:18:07.683 --> 01:18:11.645
Could the solution we're
looking for be inside us?

01:18:11.687 --> 01:18:13.397
We're humankind.

01:18:15.899 --> 01:18:19.695
Ingenuity is in our DNA,
and we can visualize.

01:18:21.571 --> 01:18:24.116
What kind of future
would you like to see?

01:18:25.200 --> 01:18:26.868
What are you willing
to contribute

01:18:26.868 --> 01:18:28.787
towards creating that future?

01:18:30.247 --> 01:18:33.041
Ask yourself, what
am I willing to do?

01:18:34.209 --> 01:18:36.837
And not something that
requires more effort

01:18:36.878 --> 01:18:38.547
than you're willing to make,

01:18:38.588 --> 01:18:40.215
and not some small contribution

01:18:40.257 --> 01:18:41.633
that just scratches
the guilt itch,

01:18:41.675 --> 01:18:43.552
but doesn't get the job done.

01:18:43.593 --> 01:18:47.139
No, ask yourself, what am
I willing to contribute

01:18:47.180 --> 01:18:49.599
that comes natural to me?

01:18:50.058 --> 01:18:51.685
Something that I can sustain

01:18:51.727 --> 01:18:54.062
until the challenge
has been met.

01:18:54.104 --> 01:18:56.606
Something that
fits into my life.

01:18:56.648 --> 01:18:58.442
My profession, my hobbies.

01:18:59.568 --> 01:19:01.778
Fits in with my relationships.

01:19:04.698 --> 01:19:07.701
Something that's a
part of who I am.

01:19:09.786 --> 01:19:13.290
Yeah, you know, each
of us, is unique.

01:19:13.331 --> 01:19:17.377
Each of us has a gift, a
strength that we can direct

01:19:17.419 --> 01:19:19.880
towards creating this world

01:19:19.921 --> 01:19:22.966
that we'd like to
see in our future.

01:19:24.426 --> 01:19:27.596
That we'd like to see in our
kids' and descendants' future.

01:19:29.598 --> 01:19:31.099
We love our kids, right?

01:19:34.394 --> 01:19:36.772
A quote from the
religious philosopher,

01:19:36.813 --> 01:19:39.691
Pierre Teilhard de
Chardin, comes to mind.

01:19:41.067 --> 01:19:45.071
"After mastering the winds,
the waves, the tides,

01:19:45.113 --> 01:19:51.119
"and gravity, we shall harness
for God the energy of love.

01:19:52.996 --> 01:19:58.001
"And then, for a second time
in the history of the world,

01:20:00.295 --> 01:20:02.130
"man will have discovered fire."

01:20:13.767 --> 01:20:18.772
[gentle electronic music]
[crickets chirp]

01:20:22.901 --> 01:20:25.695
[thunder rumbles]

01:20:37.999 --> 01:20:40.460
[birds chirp]

01:21:03.441 --> 01:21:06.111
[insect buzzes]

01:22:16.097 --> 01:22:18.767
[wind whistles]

01:22:20.352 --> 01:22:23.104
[water splashes]

